  1714	Ensure LLM Output Consistency through Formal Verification 5AI
  1715	components in general, and LLM ones in particular, can bring potential
  1716	benefits even in mission-critical systems, such as those in healthcare,
  1717	banking, law, and transportation. Systems that provide recommenda-
  1718	tions to physicians, lawyers, and financial operators based on LLMs
  1719	have already been designed [157], and one can expect that these
  1720	will become commonly used in the future. Given the criticality of
  1721	their role, these systems have to go through rigorous verification and
  1722	validation processes. The main issue for LLM-based systems compared
  1723	to traditional systems is their predictability, i.e., once a test has covered
  1724	a certain behaviour, a traditional system is expected to exhibit the same
  1725	behaviour in the future, given the same test case. Instead, for LLMs, this
  1726	cannot be fully ensured. Although one can apply redundant approaches,
  1727	such as, e.g., self-consistency [158], FM can help address the problem
  1728	in the verification process itself. In particular, abstract interpretation
  1729	can be used to approximate the behaviour of the neural network in
  1730	a way that can be mathematically analysed. This can be done by
  1731	abstracting the possible behaviour of the different activation functions
  1732	used by the neural network, e.g., ReLU, Sigmoid, and verifying the
  1733	consistency of the output in the presence of perturbations of the prompt
  1734	or other aspects that determine the variable behaviour. Contributions
  1735	in this direction have been published that apply to different neural
  1736	network architectures [159]. We argue that similar approaches can also
  1737	provide benefits for transformer architectures and LLMs. In addition,
  1738	other formal abstraction methods [160] are emerging, which help omit
  1739	specific details of a network that are not relevant to a specific feature,
  1740	thus making their verification feasible. This is particularly important for
  1741	safety-critical systems using LLM components, as these are expected to
  1742	have well-defined and restricted functionalities, which also restrict the
  1743	focus of their verification.
  1744	Action Point: Abstraction techniques used to approximate and
  1745	mathematically analyse neural network behaviour and verify output
  1746	consistency under prompt perturbations should be applied to the trans-
  1747	former architecture used in LLMs.
  1748	Regulatory Compliance at Runtime 6Regulatory compliance is con-
  1749	cerned with the assurance that a system complies with requirements
  1750	expressed in regulations, e.g., General Data Protection Regulation
  1751	(GDPR), and it is one of the central topics in RE [161]. In particular,
  1752	mission-critical systems with LLMs at their core do not only need to
  1753	exhibit repeatable behaviour but also adhere to regulations. Formal
  1754	approaches for regulatory compliance have been developed in the
  1755	past [161,162], and these can become more applicable thanks to the
  1756	usage of LLMs discussed in Section 4.
  1757	However, compliance cannot be ensured once and for all in a
  1758	context where an LLM evolves based on new knowledge on which it
  1759	may be fine-tuned, and also laws are subject to change. Even if an
  1760	LLM behaviour is made repeatable, this does not mean that the new
  1761	knowledge that can be introduced has no effect on it. In this context, the
  1762	concept of runtime verification [163], which ensures that certain prop-
  1763	erties are fulfilled during system evolution, could be adapted to address
  1764	the needs of an ever-changing regulatory and knowledge context.
  1765	Action Point: As LLMs evolve based on new knowledge and
  1766	regulations change over time, we need to adapt runtime monitoring
  1767	to continuously ensure regulatory compliance.Mitigate Bias and Address Ethical Concerns 7LLMs are subject to
  1768	many trustworthiness threats [164], including toxicity, stereotype bias,
  1769	adversarial robustness, out-of-distribution robustness, robustness on ad-
  1770	versarial demonstrations, privacy, machine ethics, and fairness. These
  1771	threats are currently measured and investigated through testing-based
  1772	approaches, such as the use of bias testing frameworks designed for SE
  1773	tasks [165]. To address the problem in a more systematic way, in line
  1774	with the guidelines presented in [166], we envision the formalisation
  1775	of a set of ethical requirements. These can be validated through formal
  1776	techniques on LLM-generated artefacts.
  1777	Action Point: Strategies for the formalisation of ethical require-
  1778	ments need to be devised, bridging the gap between value-related
  1779	concepts and the formal operationalisation of such concepts.
  1780	Summary The vision, summarised inFig. 4is structured into three
  1781	layers, a formal layer, a software (SW) artefact layer and sa LLM layer.
  1782	The LLM layer interacts with the SW artefact layer to implement the
  1783	LLM-based development process exemplified in Section 5. The LLM
  1784	layer takes as input requirements artefacts, including traditional forms
  1785	of system requirements—e.g., ‘‘shall’’ requirements or user stories—
  1786	as well as other forms, such as user feedback or issues from issue
  1787	tracking systems.12Based on these artefacts, two types of tasks can
  1788	be carried out by the LLMs: (i)analytic tasks, i.e., tasks analysing the
  1789	input and producing annotations, e.g., smells, tace-links, completeness
  1790	issues; (ii)generative tasks, i.e., tasks that produce other artefacts,
  1791	e.g., code, models, and even other requirements. These different types
  1792	of LLM-supported tasks have been exemplified in Section 5. The formal
  1793	layer enters into play to support the LLM-based development process.
  1794	In particular, formalised system requirements can be verified against
  1795	formal SW artefacts associated with LLM-generated ones 1. FM and
  1796	logical knowledge can be used for training LLMs, and external tools,
  1797	e.g., calculators and reasoners can be accessed to enhance LLM capabili-
  1798	ties2. Formal prompts can be used to better guide and orchestrate the
  1799	generative capabilities of LLM agents 3. Formal domain knowledge,
  1800	e.g., through formal ontologies, can support the generation and analysis
  1801	of domain- and project-specific artefacts and can foster explainability
  1802	4. Formal verification can also be used to verify LLMs, especially in
  1803	the case of mission-critical systems 5. Runtime verification can be
  1804	used to facilitate the monitoring of regulatory requirements in the pres-
  1805	ence of LLM evolution triggered by new knowledge 6. Finally, formal
  1806	system artefacts can be verified against formalised ethical requirements
  1807	7.
  1808	7. Practical considerations and limitations
  1809	The two roadmaps outlined offer a vision of how FMs and LLMs
  1810	could be integrated into future software development processes. The
  1811	vision is unavoidably non-exhaustive, and the actual implementation
  1812	of the different research directions requires careful planning to prevent
  1813	risks and address limitations. In the following, we outline practical
  1814	considerations regarding the implementation of the roadmaps, as well
  1815	as possible barriers to its unfolding.
  1816	Collaboration Between LLM and FM Experts LLMs and FMs are highly
  1817	specialised fields that require significant expertise to fully master their
  1818	technical intricacies and utilise their tools effectively. Successful imple-
  1819	mentation of the envisioned roadmaps also depends on collaboration
  1820	and clear communication between LLM experts and FM experts. These
  1821	specialists, though, often come from different schools of thought (statis-
  1822	tical vs deterministic thinking), making interdisciplinary collaboration
  1823	challenging. Facilitating this cooperation falls within the domain of
  1824	requirements engineers, who play a key role in bridging the gap be-
  1825	tween these diverse fields. A similar observation was made by Franch
  1826	et al. [167], when speaking about the role of requirements engineers in
  1827	12The LLM layer can also take other SW artefacts as input, but here we
  1828	mainly focus on a RE-centred process, to simplify the figure and the narrative.
  1829
  1830	--- Page 17 ---
  1831	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
  1832	17A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
  1833	the development of systems with artificial intelligence (AI) components
  1834	at their core.
  1835	Empirical Evaluation As in any type of research endeavour, empirical
  1836	evaluation is crucial to assess the effectiveness and reliability of the
  1837	approaches being developed. The use of generative AI solutions like
  1838	LLMs poses challenges in RE due to the lack of datasets for evaluation.
  1839	This issue becomes even more pronounced when FMs are also involved,
  1840	as the empirical maturity of the FM field remains limited, particularly
  1841	when considering their application in practical settings [168]. Further-
  1842	more, some generative capabilities of LLMs cannot be evaluated using
  1843	pre-defined ground truths. For instance, it is not feasible to assume that
  1844	there is a single correct formal specification for a given piece of code,
  1845	or vice versa. In such cases, qualitative evaluation methods, typically
  1846	employed in interview studies or case studies—–such as thematic anal-
  1847	ysis [169] or grounded theory [170]—–should be incorporated, as also
  1848	done by Ferrari et al. [9]. With these methods, one can qualitatively yet
  1849	scientifically check the output of the LLMs, and assess its quality in a
  1850	critical way without relying on a pre-defined ground truth. Facilitating
  1851	the acceptance of these research methods in fields that typically rely
  1852	on quantitative evaluation approaches, such as LLMs, or adhere strictly
  1853	to positivist principles, such as FMs, remains a significant hurdle to
  1854	overcome.
  1855	Overreliance on LLM Output LLMs can notoriously hallucinate and
  1856	produce incorrect outputs in response to a given prompt [171]. How-
  1857	ever, the generated output often appears plausible, and the verbose
  1858	explanations provided by LLMs, such as ChatGPT, can convey a sense of
  1859	machine self-confidence that may mislead a requirements engineer. As
  1860	a result, there is a risk of over-reliance on the generated content. While
  1861	the integration of FMs in the software process aims to mitigate this
  1862	issue with formal prompts and formal verification, additional measures
  1863	are necessary to ensure that users do not blindly trust the output
  1864	of LLMs. These include but are not limited to: (a) the definition of
  1865	novel human-centred quality control mechanisms; (b) the identification
  1866	and prevention of typical hallucinatory patterns; and (c) development
  1867	processes specifically tailored for LLM development and training. These
  1868	are expected to test the LLMs’ robustness before they are deployed in
  1869	software development toolchains such as the ones envisioned in this
  1870	paper (cf.Fig. 2and4).
  1871	Diminishing Role of Human Creativity In the figures of our roadmaps,
  1872	we deliberately excluded human actors to emphasise the primary role
  1873	of automated tools, and somehow also provoke requirements and soft-
  1874	ware engineers to find their position in the overall picture(s). However,
  1875	with LLMs taking over creative aspects of software development, the
  1876	role of humans risks being reduced to mere supervision, depriving
  1877	them of the satisfaction and intellectual engagement that typically
  1878	comes from the hands-on process of software creation. In the past,
  1879	automation in software engineering was oriented to address tedious
  1880	and error prone tasks, such as requirements review or tracing. This was
  1881	expected to leave more space for requirements elicitation, modelling,
  1882	and coding, which are arguably more exciting and less bureaucratic
  1883	tasks. In a context such as the one envisioned, in which LLMs take
  1884	all the interesting jobs, one could question the role of requirements
  1885	engineers. We argue that requirements engineers, which are often
  1886	neglected in software development [172], will actually become central.
  1887	As software development becomes increasingly automated with the use
  1888	of LLMs, requirements will emerge as the primary means of interacting
  1889	with these models, especially for coding tasks. Indeed, in processes
  1890	like the one outlined in Section 5, the inputs are the requirements,
  1891	and the output is the code. As a result, software engineers will need
  1892	to transition into roles as requirements engineers, orchestrating the
  1893	entire development process through the management and specification
  1894	of requirements.
  1895	Limited Training on FM datasets As briefly touched in Section 4, one
  1896	challenge to overcome is that LLMs have been mainly trained on NL
  1897	data, and their knowledge of formal languages may be limited. This
  1898	especially affects the generation of formal artefacts from conventionalones and vice versa. Some ideas to deal with this aspect have already
  1899	been mentioned in the paper. One can rely on the similarity between
  1900	some formal languages and programming languages, and the code
  1901	synthesis ability of LLMs specialised on code [113,114]. Fine-tuning
  1902	LLMs on FM data is also a possibility, as well as integrating the output
  1903	of different LLMs trained or fine-tuned on different types of data (NL,
  1904	code, FM). Finally, one research avenue is an interactive generation,
  1905	in which the human guides the generation of formal or conventional
  1906	artefacts through iteration of prompts, answers, and corrections. This
  1907	can possibly be further automated with an agent-based paradigm that
  1908	enables LLMs to access model checkers and compilers, to assess the
  1909	syntax correctness of the generated artefacts.
  1910	Proliferation and Maintainability of Artefacts Combining LLMs and
  1911	FMs is expected, on the one hand, to speed up the development process
  1912	and, on the other hand, to foster system correctness. At the same time,
  1913	these advantages also come with more artefacts to maintain and keep
  1914	consistent, i.e., more code, but also more models, formulae, counterex-
  1915	amples, etc. LLM-based automatic trace link generation, as discussed in
  1916	Section 4, can help cope with artefact evolution, but the proliferation
  1917	of the trace link themselves can become hardly manageable. Novel
  1918	techniques are thus required to better monitor the evolution and control
  1919	the increased pace and complexity of development. We argue that the
  1920	software visualisation community can help in this direction [173]. Soft-
  1921	ware visualisation tools can facilitate real-time monitoring of artefact
  1922	changes, enabling teams to quickly identify inconsistencies and assess
  1923	the impact of modifications across the system. Furthermore, integrating
  1924	visualisation with analytics can provide predictive insights, helping
  1925	teams anticipate potential issues arising from the rapid evolution of
  1926	artefacts.
  1927	Deployment, Scalability and Technological Evolution
  1928	Radically changing an established software development approach
  1929	by introducing FMs and LLMs is not a quick procedure, and requires
  1930	careful consideration and planning. Research is needed not only to
  1931	explore novel LLM-based and FM-controlled software processes but also
  1932	to define effective strategies fordeploying these processes in real-world
  1933	software development contexts. To facilitate this transition, incremen-
  1934	tal process transformation approaches should be implemented, allowing
  1935	for smooth integration and fostering acceptance among development
  1936	teams.
  1937	Another significant challenge to address is thescalability of the
  1938	proposed ideas. FMs, particularly model checkers, often face the state
  1939	space explosion problem, which can complicate the verification of large
  1940	systems. On the other hand, LLMs require substantial computational
  1941	resources for execution, which can be a barrier to their widespread
  1942	adoption. Solutions such as statistical model checking [174] or more
  1943	recent proposals such as quantum probabilistic model checking [175],
  1944	besides other techniques, can help mitigate the state space explosion
  1945	issue. Furthermore, model distillation approaches [28] can reduce the
  1946	resource demands associated with LLMs.
  1947	Lastly, the pace oftechnological evolution presents a unique chal-
  1948	lenge. The world of FMs traditionally develops industry-ready tools
  1949	slowly, allowing companies ample time to adopt them. In contrast, new
  1950	LLMs are emerging daily, which can overwhelm teams trying to keep up
  1951	with the rapid advancements. Therefore, flexible approaches that can
  1952	accommodate ongoing technological changes are essential. By address-
  1953	ing these challenges, the integration of FMs and LLMs into software
  1954	development can be achieved in a manner that is both practical and
  1955	sustainable.
  1956	8. Conclusion
  1957	We outlined two roadmaps: one aimed at ensuring the reliable
  1958	use of LLMs in RE activities, and the other focused on enhancing the
  1959	usability of formal RE through LLM support. On the one hand, our
  1960	vision reimagines the application of formal RE by leveraging LLMs to
  1961	make formal languages and tools more accessible, and on the other, the
  1962
  1963	--- Page 18 ---
  1964	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
  1965	18A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
  1966	proposed use of formal techniques when LLMs are used in RE activities
  1967	could mitigate concerns related to LLM-generated artefacts and estab-
  1968	lish a framework for responsible and trustworthy AI. The roadmaps are
  1969	well-suited for rigorous software engineering processes, such as the V-
  1970	process for mission-critical systems, where formal methods can play a
  1971	key role in ensuring reliability and trustworthiness. However, they are
  1972	not restricted to such approaches, as we believe that greater formality
  1973	can provide benefits in any development context. This observation is
  1974	especially important in a world in which safety, security, privacy, and
  1975	ethical requirements are becoming increasingly relevant across many
  1976	types of applications.
  1977	The primary aim of the roadmaps is to stimulate further research
  1978	and reflection, while the authors will pursue part of the outlined
  1979	directions. In the context of the roadmaps, our main focus will be on: (i)
  1980	translating requirements into formal logic using LLMs, building on prior
  1981	research initiated by the authors before the introduction of LLMs [131];
  1982	(ii) exploring the generation and analysis of software artefacts with
  1983	LLMs, a line of work already being developed by one of the authors [9];
  1984	and (iii) leveraging LLMs to explain formal artefacts, which we identify
  1985	as a critical challenge for the broader adoption of FMs. We hope that
  1986	our roadmaps will inspire our readers to take over the other research
  1987	avenues, and possibly develop new, unforeseen ones.
