   920	Listing 4: Python code for Receiver
   921	We note that some details of the code (e.g., host, port, socket) did
   922	not appear in the original model, although the abstract behaviour is the
   923	same. For this reason, additional formal verification may be required.
   924	Phyton code can be verified directly by Dafny [106], a programming
   925	language and verification tool designed to enable formal verification
   926	of program correctness by incorporating specifications directly into
   927	the code. While Dafny is not natively designed to verify Python code,
   928	critical sections of Python code can be translated into Dafny, specifying
   929	the required properties and using Dafny to perform the verification.
   930	Phyton programs can also be directly analysed using static analysis
   931	with tools such as Nagini [112] that can verify memory safety, func-
   932	tional properties, termination, deadlock freedom, and input/output
   933	behaviour.
   934	4. Roadmap: Using LLMs to support FM-based development
   935	The example of Section 3gives an idea of what could be a typical
   936	formal RE-based development context, in which formal logics are used
   937	to express requirements, formal models define the specification, and
   938	formal tools verify the specification against its requirements. We argue
   939	that the different steps of the process can be supported by LLMs, and
   940	their application can facilitate the uptake of FMs in practice, seamlessly
   941	integrating them into standard development processes. For example,
   942	LLMs can replace the human in the translation from the model in
   943	Listing 1into the code of Listings 3and4. Analogously, LLMs can
   944	translate the different requirements into Spinassert (as used in the
   945	example for the Flow Control requirement), or Spin never claims for
   946	global requirements or liveness properties. In the following, we discuss
   947	different possible usages of LLMs in a formal RE-based development
   948	process, and discuss research avenues, also summarising the main
   949	action points (highlighted with
   950	 ).Fig. 2summarises and connects the
   951	different discussion topics presented in the rest of this section. Each
   952	discussion topic is associated with a circled number, which also appears
   953	in the figure. We will describe the figure at the end of the section to
   954	provide a comprehensive view of the roadmap.
   955	Generating FM and SE Artifacts 1In a development context in which
   956	one first develops a formal specification and then implements the
   957	software, LLMs can provide support for code generation from formal
   958	specifications. One can assume that this task can be easily addressed
   959	by LLMs, considering their ability in code synthesis—see, e.g., Code
   960	Llama [113] or StarCoder [114]. A specification is more abstract than
   961	the desired code—e.g., including specific library functions to create and
   962	connect sockets, as in Listings 3and 4—and one wishes to identify the
   963	correct project-dependent or machine-dependent function calls to be
   964	included in the code.
   965	To generate code from a formal specification identifying the correct
   966	source code functions can, in principle, be achieved through RAG
   967	approaches, in which an existing knowledge base is exploited to aug-
   968	ment a prompt with information to be used to better inform the LLM.
   969	In this case, the knowledge base would be composed of the existing
   970	libraries that can be used in the program to be synthesised. This is the
   971	approach adopted by the recent preliminary contribution by Kozioklek
   972	et al. [115], where the authors use RAG to enrich generated code
   973	with proprietary and well-tested function blocks in the field of control
   974	systems.
   975	Action Point: To facilitate code generation from formal specifi-
   976	cations, RAG can be employed. By enriching prompts with code from
   977	existing libraries, the generated output can integrate these resources.
