   982	Fig. 2.Overall view of the salient points of the roadmap  on using LLM agents to support FM-based  development.
   983	Another  natural  usage of LLMs is the generation  of formal  spec-
   984	ifications  and logic formulae  based on code and requirements,  re-
   985	spectively.  Considering  our example  in Section 3, this means  starting
   986	from the source  code in Listing 3and4to produce  the model in
   987	Listing 1, or starting  from the initial requirements  to produce  asserts
   988	or never claims.  The challenge  here is the opposite  one discussed
   989	for specification-to-code  translation.  Since the source  code is typically
   990	more refined  than a formal  specification,  the mechanism  of translation
   991	of source  code into a specification  needs to be able to abstract  from
   992	implementation  details.  This is also needed  to ensure  that formal  verifi-
   993	cation is feasible  with the given memory  resources  and time constraints,
   994	thus avoiding  the state-space  explosion  problem,  which typically  occurs
   995	when performing  model-checking  of large specifications.  Automatically
   996	identifying  the relevant  backbone  of a code module  or a complex
   997	program  can be challenging.  LLMs have demonstrated  a strong  ability
   998	in summarising  code [116], which means  generating  short functional
   999	descriptions  of code snippets.  This suggests  a strong  potential  in ab-
  1000	stracting  the meaning  of source  code, which can, in principle,  be
  1001	exploited  also to support  the creation  of formal  specifications.
  1002	While works exist that investigate  the performance  LLM for gener-
  1003	ating graphical  models  from NL requirements  [9], we are not aware
  1004	of studies  targeting  the generation  of formal  high-level  models  similar
  1005	to the one in Listing 1from source  code. It is worth noting  that
  1006	software  model checking  [117] is a technique  that allows  creating
  1007	abstract  models  from source  code and verifying implementation-related
  1008	properties,  e.g., memory-safety,  array bounds,  and runtime  errors in
  1009	general.  Instead,  traditional  model checking,  as the one exemplified  in
  1010	Section 3, considers  a more high-level  model to verify other types of
  1011	conceptual properties,  e.g., safety,  liveness,  and concurrency.  Generat-
  1012	ing these types of high-level  models  could be beneficial  to ensure  that
  1013	the code verifies  conceptual  properties.  We argue that this could be an
  1014	interesting  avenue  of research,  which could facilitate  both the devel-
  1015	opment  and maintenance  of formal  specifications,  and the support  of
  1016	legacy  systems  that often lack specifications  and documentation.  LLMs
  1017	may have limited  knowledge  of formal  languages  due to the modest
  1018	FM data used during  their training.  However,  the similarity  between
  1019	some formal  languages  and certain  programming  languages  (e.g., thesimilarity  between  C code and PROMELA  code) could facilitate  the
  1020	translation.
  1021	Action  Point: To generate  formal  specifications  from code or
  1022	requirements,  we can use LLMs summarisation  capabilities  to abstract
  1023	from source  code and extract  essential  functional  descriptions  for for-
  1024	mal models.  Similarities  between  programming  languages  and formal
  1025	languages  can overcome  the problem  of limited  FM knowledge  of LLMs.
  1026	Concerning  the generation  of logic formulae  from NL requirements
  1027	using LLMs, Cosler  et al. [118] recently  introduced nl2spec , a tool
  1028	for translating  NL expressions  into LTL formulae.  The tool addresses
  1029	the inherent  ambiguity  of NL through  human  interaction.  Besides  the
  1030	formula,  it also generates  translations  of sub-expressions  of the orig-
  1031	inal one, which helps the user identify  possible  mistakes  and apply
  1032	corrections.  However,  LTL is only one of the many temporal  logics
  1033	that cannot  express  all the types of real-world  requirements,  and more
  1034	effort is needed  to have a comprehensive  environment  for requirements
  1035	translation.  Initiatives  such as the Natural2CTL  dataset  [119], which
  1036	includes  pairs of NL properties  and their translation  into Computation
  1037	Tree Logic (CTL),  can be particularly  beneficial  for the community,
  1038	as they can be used to fine-tune  LLMs and test their performance  on
  1039	the translation  task. The translation  of NL into logic, independently
  1040	from the chosen  type of logic, can be achieved  by first addressing  the
  1041	ambiguity  of NL and then generating  the equivalent  formula.  This can
  1042	be coupled  with providing  explanations  to enable  the identification  of
  1043	possible  mistakes,  as well as the rationale  behind  the translation.
  1044	Action  Point: To translate  NL requirements  into logic, we need
  1045	to: (1) provide  tools that iterate  with users to reduce  NL ambiguity;
  1046	(2) provide  and leverage  specialised  datasets  with requirement-formula
  1047	pairs; and (3) enrich  translations  with explanations.
  1048	Explaining  FM Artifacts 2The formal  model illustrated  in the exam-
  1049	ple, as well as the LTL formula,  are rather simple  to understand  without
  1050	knowing  all the nuances  of formal  languages  and logic. However,  for-
  1051	mal requirements  and specifications  can easily become  more complex
  1052	and thus hard to interpret.  This occurs  especially  when real-world
  1053	systems  are modelled  and when complex  properties  need to be ex-
  1054	pressed,  possibly  involving  real-time  constraints,  e.g., using Real-time
  1055	CTL [120], or recursion/iterations,  e.g., using 𝜇-calculus  [121]. The
  1056
  1057	--- Page 10 ---
  1058	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
  1059	10A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
  1060	reader is invited to look at the following repositories, which collect for-
  1061	mal specifications and formulae in different formal languages.678[122–
  1062	124]. Some of the specifications are single files with over 2 K lines of
  1063	code that could benefit from an explanation, even for someone who
  1064	is highly familiar with the adopted formal languages. This problem is
  1065	further exacerbated by the lack of support for modularity of some of the
  1066	languages [125]. Explainability is one of the ten principles that an FM
  1067	needs to satisfy to be applicable, according to Gleirscher et al. [126].
  1068	In fact, domain experts from companies who wish to use FMs do
  1069	not typically need to develop formal specifications themselves and
  1070	normally rely on external consultants [127]. It is thus important that
  1071	they can understand the specifications to ensure that the information
  1072	about the system to be modelled, verified, and developed is correctly
  1073	conveyed. Explainability is also an important feature for the FM experts
  1074	themselves, especially when dealing with counterexamples generated
  1075	by model checkers. These can be rather complex, and some engines
  1076	offer simulation capabilities to execute the counterexamples [128] and
  1077	visualise the traces that violate a certain property. However, these
  1078	simulations could also benefit from NL language explanations that
  1079	describe, e.g., the transitions activated in a model or the statements
  1080	executed.
  1081	LLMs can be used to automatically provide NL explanations for
  1082	models, formulae, and counterexamples. Again considering our exam-
  1083	ple in Section 3, this could entail: including a summary description
  1084	of the model, as well as appropriate comments; explain theassert
  1085	(e.g., ‘‘The currently received message is the one currently generated
  1086	by the sender. This prevents overwhelming the receiver’’.); explain the
  1087	counterexample inFig. 1(e.g., ‘‘The Sender can generate more than
  1088	a message (message 2 and 3) when the Receiver is still processing an
  1089	old message (message 1)’’). A peculiarity of formal languages is that
  1090	these can have different levels of formality, e.g., formal, such as timed
  1091	automata, or semi-formal, such as UML. The former can be hard to
  1092	understand, while the latter can lead to different interpretations. In
  1093	both cases, NL explanations become crucial. Approaches for code com-
  1094	ments generation have been recently developed [129], and we argue
  1095	that similar solutions could also apply to formal specifications. The idea
  1096	can be further evolved by taking inspiration from LLM-based solutions
  1097	for code explanation, such as the one from Nam et al. [130]. This
  1098	method enhances code comprehension by providing localised textual
  1099	illustrations of the code content directly within the development envi-
  1100	ronment. Concerning explanations of formulae, while works have been
  1101	performed to translate NL requirements into formal logics [118], tools
  1102	that explain formulae to non-experts using LLMs are lacking. To our
  1103	knowledge, preliminary studies have been performed only using more
  1104	traditional deep learning approaches for machine translation [131].
  1105	Concerning counterexamples, these can be particularly complex and
  1106	hard to follow. However, these are not too dissimilar from stack traces
  1107	generated by faulty code. Preliminary approaches for explaining stack
  1108	traces via LLMs have been recently developed [132], and similar solu-
  1109	tions can also be applied to support the navigation of counterexamples
  1110	through the simulation of faulty formal specifications.
  1111	Action Point: Explanations of FM artifacts can be achieved by
  1112	leveraging techniques from code comment generation, localised textual
  1113	illustrations, and stack trace analysis.
  1114	Translating Formal Languages 3As noticed by previous work, each
  1115	formal language and environment has its own unique peculiarities [127,
  1116	133]. Some focus on quantitative requirements (e.g., performance,
  1117	availability), some on qualitative ones (e.g., safety, security); some
  1118	provide friendly GUIs and visual simulation, some others offer support
  1119	for code generation, etc.—see [134] for a systematic evaluation of
  1120	13 formal tools. A formal RE-based development process should be
  1121	6https://github.com/hhu-stups/specifications Last visit 22 Oct. 2024.
  1122	7https://www.rers-challenge.org/2018/ . Last visit 22 Oct. 2024.
  1123	8https://qcomp.org/benchmarks/ . Last visit 22 Oct. 2024.able to integrate different languages and tools to address the diverse
  1124	dimensions of the system to be verified, and provide models that target
  1125	different audiences, e.g., Simulink models for practitioners vsSpin
  1126	models for FM experts. This was also observed in [135], where Real-
  1127	Time Graphical Interval Logic templates are compositionally encoded in
  1128	different temporal logics, thus enabling the portability of specifications.
  1129	To this end, FM practitioners should be able to automatically translate
  1130	specifications from one language to another to guarantee consistency
  1131	between the models used by the tools, or to provide different views
  1132	of the system—for example, by omitting or adding details to achieve
  1133	different levels of abstraction—–even when using the same source
  1134	and target language. Consistency between models is important to
  1135	ensure FM diversity [136], which provides further guarantees of the
  1136	soundness of the verification process, especially in the presence of tools
  1137	that have not been certified and can thus generate incorrect output
  1138	despite their formal foundations. LLMs have demonstrated abilities in
  1139	code translation for different programming languages [137]. This code
  1140	translation technique can be fruitfully exploited also in a scenario in
  1141	which different FMs are adopted in system development to address
  1142	different concerns and verify multiple types of properties.
  1143	Action Point: To translate formal languages into one another,
  1144	thus adapting models to different verification environments and au-
  1145	diences, the code-to-code translation capabilities of LLMs can be ex-
  1146	ploited.
  1147	Supporting Iterations and Evolution 4The simple example provided
  1148	assumes that starting from requirements, one passes to models and
  1149	formulae to be verified and then to code. However, like any system
  1150	development process, a formal RE-based one is also incremental and
  1151	iterative. One can start from early NL requirements or even high-
  1152	level system descriptions and then provide specifications, which are
  1153	then refined, together with the requirements, and eventually translated
  1154	into code. The code and the system as a whole—possibly including
  1155	hardware and communication components—go through different levels
  1156	of testing (unit, integration, acceptance, etc.), possibly through the
  1157	support of FMs for test generation such as model-based testing [138].
  1158	In a rigorous development process, this creates incremental versions
  1159	of requirements documents, specifications, tests, and other process
  1160	artefacts, which need to be kept aligned with trace-links to ensure
  1161	consistency across artefacts. Trace-link identification has been a long-
  1162	standing NLP problem [139]. Given the ability of LLMs to interpret both
  1163	NL expressions and code, we argue that a combination of code-specific
  1164	LLMs and NL-oriented ones can be beneficial in taking a step forward
  1165	in addressing this challenging problem. Early studies in this direction
  1166	have already been performed by Rodriguez et al. [6].
  1167	Action Point: To support iterations and evolution, we can lever-
  1168	age a combination of code-specific and NL-oriented LLMs to enhance
  1169	trace-link identification and ensure consistency across all artefacts
  1170	produced in the development process.
  1171	Automating Knowledge Engineering 5Knowledge engineering is the
  1172	collection of activities for eliciting, capturing, conceptualising, and
  1173	formalising knowledge to be used in information systems. It provides
  1174	support for formal RE activities, especially in creating and analysing
  1175	models, and typically utilises formal or semi-formal representation
  1176	languages. As noticed in [140], LLMs can significantly streamline the
  1177	process of extracting and structuring knowledge from unstructured data
  1178	sources. By leveraging LLMs, systems could autonomously identify,
  1179	extract, and integrate relevant information, reducing the reliance on
  1180	manual input and expertise. Analogously, Mateiu and Groza [141]
  1181	emphasise the role of LLMs in ontology engineering, where these
  1182	models can aid in creating, updating, and refining ontologies. This
  1183	supports more dynamic and up-to-date knowledge bases. LLMs also
  1184	have the potential to improve the semantic understanding of sys-
  1185	tems [142] by enhancing the ability of systems to understand and
  1186	reason about complex, context-dependent information, which is crucial
