  1201	tured, software  engineering  artefacts.  Ontologies  can in turn be used
  1202	to support  LLM-driven  development  (cf. Section 5).
  1203	Summary The vision,  summarised  inFig. 2, is structured  into three
  1204	interconnected  layers,9a formal  development  layer, a conventional
  1205	development  layer and an LLM layer, which is composed  by different
  1206	LLM agents.  In the original  formal  development  process  exemplified  in
  1207	Section 3, the interaction  between  the two layers is manual,  i.e., one
  1208	needs to manually  translate  models  and logic formulae  from/to  code
  1209	and requirements,  respectively.  In the envisioned  process,  the inter-
  1210	action is delegated  to LLMs. In particular,  conventional  and formal
  1211	development  layers exploit  different  LLMs for translating  conventional
  1212	artefacts  into formal  ones, and vice versa 1. Formal  artefacts  can be
  1213	explained  with LLMs 2, while other LLMs can provide  model-to-model
  1214	transformations 3. The LLM layer can also contribute  by creating  trace
  1215	links between  all the artefacts  of the two layers,  thus supporting  artefact
  1216	evolution 4. Finally,  process  and domain  knowledge  can be extracted
  1217	9Here, layers should be intended  as graphical  layers, similar to those in
  1218	Photoshop,  i.e., distinct  groups of elements  that exist at different  depths within
  1219	the composition,  with some forming  the foreground  (Formal  Development
  1220	Layer and Conventional  Development  Layer) and others receding  into the
  1221	background  (LLM Layer).  This applies  also toFig. 4.from the different  artefacts  through  LLMs, and can be further  exploited
  1222	by the different  development  layers and LLMs 5.
  1223	5. LLM-driven  software  development:  an example
  1224	LLMs can be used to support  the RE and SE process,  and different
  1225	works have been published  in this sense, mainly  focusing  on code
  1226	activities  such as code generation  and testing  [2,3]. In the following,
  1227	we exemplify  a possible  RE-centred  development  process  in which
  1228	requirements  can be generated  from high-level  descriptions,  analysed
  1229	for ambiguity  and completeness,  improved,  classified,  and traced  with
  1230	other requirements.  LLMs can also be used to automatically  generate
  1231	graphical  models,  code, comments,  and tests. Once an app has been
  1232	released,  user feedback  can be analysed  to improve  the requirements.
  1233	In the following,  we exemplify  prompts  and associated  LLM output
  1234	to implement  a process  composed  of the steps outlined  above.  To
  1235	generate  the output,  we have prompted  the online  interface  of ChatGPT
  1236	3.5 and slightly  adjusted  the results  to make them more synthetic.
  1237	We want to remark  that the adjustments  were limited,  although  some
  1238	iterative  prompting  was required  in some cases. In particular,  we
  1239	reduced  the verbose  replies  typical  of ChatGPT,  and had to iterate  a
  1240	few times before  obtaining  a clear and faithful  diagram  such as the
  1241	one inFig. 3, prompting  the LLM to add or remove  details.  To make
  1242	our storytelling  more natural,  we will not strictly  follow  the sequence
  1243	of steps. Furthermore,  we will consider  different  types of systems,  as
  1244	not all tasks are appropriate  for all systems.
