  1280	User Feedback Analysis Let us now imagine that we have developed
  1281	the app, we have released it to the public, and we want to summarise
  1282	the feedback from users. We can imagine using a RAG system to
  1283	retrieve users’ feedback and trigger the LLM with the following prompt:
  1284	Please act as an expert in summarising user feedback. What are the main
  1285	concerns of the users? Please associate each concern with a non-functional
  1286	requirement class.
  1287	∙Accuracy: Some users complained that the app is not
  1288	accurate enough for tuning their guitars, especially in noisy
  1289	environments or with alternate tunings. This could affect
  1290	the usability and reliability of the app.
  1291	∙Performance: Some users reported that the app is slow to
  1292	load, crashes frequently, or drains the battery too fast. This
  1293	could affect the efficiency and availability of the app.
  1294	∙Security: Some users expressed concerns about the app’s
  1295	data collection and privacy policies, especially regarding
  1296	microphone access and location tracking. This could affect
  1297	the confidentiality and integrity of the app.
  1298	∙Compatibility: Some users mentioned that the app is not
  1299	compatible with their devices, operating systems, or other
  1300	apps. This could affect the portability and interoperability
  1301	of the app.
  1302	∙Cost: Some users felt that the app is too expensive for
  1303	its features, or that the subscription model is not fair
  1304	or transparent. This could affect the affordability and
  1305	maintainability of the app.
  1306	Smell Detection In the development process of mission-critical systems,
  1307	it is typical to perform requirements reviews to identify ambiguities
  1308	and smells in general. Let us consider the following prompt: Please
  1309	act as an ambiguity detection tool for requirements. Does the followingrequirement contain any ambiguities? Requirement: The onboard device
  1310	sends a connection request to the wayside and monitoring subsystem, and it
  1311	responds with an acknowledgement or aborts the connection.
  1312	The requirement contains a potential ambiguity:
  1313	It responds with an acknowledgment or aborts the con-
  1314	nection: The ambiguity here lies in what exactly is meant by ‘‘it’’.
  1315	Does ‘‘it’’ refer to the onboard device, the wayside and monitoring
  1316	subsystem, or some other entity? It’s unclear which component is
  1317	responsible for responding with the acknowledgment or abort the
  1318	action.
  1319	To clarify this requirement , you might want to specify which
  1320	entity is responsible for sending the acknowledgment or initi-
  1321	ating the connection termination. For example, ‘‘The onboard
  1322	device sends a connection request to the wayside and monitor-
  1323	ing subsystem. The subsystem responds with an acknowledgment
  1324	if successful, or it initiates an abort of the connection request
  1325	if necessary’’. This clarification makes it clear which entity is
  1326	performing each action.
  1327	The LLM identifies a so-called anaphoric ambiguity , due to a pronoun
  1328	for which it is unclear which is the referent. Not always anaphorae
  1329	lead to ambiguity, and the literature distinguishes between nocuous and
  1330	innocuous ambiguities [143]. The former are the ones for which a reader
  1331	can easily find the referent, while the latter are the ones that may cause
  1332	misunderstandings. LLMs are expected to have a deep understanding
  1333	of the semantics of a text and can show the capability to recognise
  1334	innocuous ambiguities. Let us consider the following prompt, including
  1335	a rephrasing of the previous requirement: Please act as an ambiguity
  1336	detection tool. Is this requirement ambiguous? Requirement: When the
  1337	system receives a corrupted message, it terminates the connection with an
  1338	abort message.
  1339	The requirement seems relatively clear and straightforward,
  1340	but there is a minor potential ambiguity that could be clarified:
  1341	‘‘Corrupted message’’: The term could be interpreted in multi-
  1342	ple ways. Does it refer to a message with invalid syntax, a message
  1343	with incorrect data, or a message that has been tampered with
  1344	during transmission? Providing a precise definition or clarification
  1345	of what constitutes a ‘‘corrupted message’’ would help eliminate
  1346	any ambiguity.
  1347	In this case, the pronoun is not recognised as a source of ambiguity,
  1348	as the referent can clearly be inferred from the context (it is an
  1349	innocuous ambiguity ). On the other hand, the ambiguity detection LLM
  1350	is able to recognise a case of generality, i.e., an expression that is too
  1351	abstract and can be interpreted in different ways. Similar to this case,
  1352	one can analyse other requirements defects, such as inconsistency and
  1353	incompleteness. This second case is demonstrated in the following.
  1354	Completeness Check and Requirements Completion Before proceeding
  1355	with development, it is important to ensure that requirements are
  1356	sufficiently complete. In this case, let us consider a larger set of re-
  1357	quirements for a railway subsystem function that enables the so-called
  1358	procedure of red-crossing, i.e., a procedure that allows the driver to
  1359	cross a red signal after receiving authorisation from the infrastructure
  1360	manager. The prompt is reported in the following framed box.
