  1376	Red Crossing Procedure.
  1377	3. If the driver decides to perform the Red Crossing pro-
  1378	cedure, it first needs to receive authorisation from the
  1379	infrastructure manager.
  1380	4. Then, after the authorisation, the driver can press the Red
  1381	Crossing button for at least 5 s and less than 10 s.
  1382	5. After 5 s, a light blinks on the panel.
  1383	6. When the light blinks, the driver can perform the red
  1384	crossing.
  1385	7. The driver shall perform the red crossing within 30 s after
  1386	the light blinks.
  1387	8. If the red signal is not crossed within the 30 s, the onboard
  1388	system shall automatically stop the train.
  1389	9. After 30 s from the moment in which it was lighted on, the
  1390	light stops blinking.
  1391	The generated output is:
  1392	The requirements that you provided are not sufficiently com-
  1393	plete to make sense of the system’s behaviour. There are some
  1394	gaps that need to be resolved. Here are some ways to modify the
  1395	requirements to complete them:
  1396	At the requirement level , you should specify the conditions
  1397	and actions more clearly and consistently. For example, in re-
  1398	quirement 2, you should state what happens if the driver does not
  1399	press the red-crossing button or does not perform the red-crossing
  1400	procedure. In requirement 4, you should state what happens if
  1401	the driver presses the red crossing button for less than 5 s or
  1402	more than 10 s. In requirement 6, you should state how the driver
  1403	performs the red crossing (e.g., by pressing a pedal or a lever).
  1404	At the requirements set level , you should add some re-
  1405	quirements that are missing or implied by the existing ones. For
  1406	example, you should state what is the purpose and scope of
  1407	the system, what are the roles and responsibilities of the driver
  1408	and the infrastructure manager, what are the assumptions and
  1409	constraints of the system, what are the expected outcomes and
  1410	quality attributes of the system, and what are the exceptions and
  1411	error handling mechanisms of the system.
  1412	We can then ask the LLM to complete the requirements. Prompt:
  1413	Please rewrite the requirements so that they can be considered complete.∙Architectural Requirements
  1414	(a) The system shall provide a red signal crossing per-
  1415	mission function for the driver of the train when the
  1416	train encounters a red signal on the railway track.
  1417	(b) The system shall consist of an onboard system and
  1418	a wayside system that communicate with each other
  1419	via wireless signals.
  1420	(c) The onboard system shall consist of a dashboard, a
  1421	red crossing button, a light indicator, and a brake
  1422	controller.
  1423	∙Functional Requirements
  1424	(1) When the train encounters a red signal, the onboard
  1425	system shall display a red signal icon on the dash-
  1426	board, and the driver shall stop the train before the
  1427	signal.
  1428	(2) After the driver stops the train, the driver can either
  1429	wait for a green signal or request a red crossing per-
  1430	mission from the infrastructure manager by pressing
  1431	and holding the red crossing button for at least 5 s
  1432	and less than 10 s.
  1433	(3) If the driver presses and holds the red crossing
  1434	button for less than 5 s or more than 10 s, the
  1435	onboard system shall display an error message on
  1436	the dashboard and ignore the request.
  1437	(4) If the driver presses and holds the red crossing but-
  1438	ton for at least 5 s and less than 10 s, the onboard
  1439	system shall send a request message to the way-
  1440	side system and display a waiting message on the
  1441	dashboard.
  1442	(5) The wayside system shall receive the request mes-
  1443	sage from the onboard system and forward it to the
  1444	infrastructure manager station.
  1445	(6) The infrastructure manager shall review the request
  1446	message and decide whether to grant or deny the
  1447	red crossing permission based on the safety and
  1448	operational conditions of the railway track.
  1449	(7) The infrastructure manager shall send a response
  1450	message to the wayside system indicating the
  1451	decision and the reason.
  1452	The LLM not only creates a list of requirements that better specify
  1453	the expected behaviour but also includes architectural requirements, as
  1454	well as the requirements for the infrastructure manager (the last two
  1455	requirements), which were not considered in the initial requirements
  1456	set.
  1457	Model Generation When requirements have been defined, one passes to
  1458	the system design, which frequently leverages the support of graphical
  1459	representations, such as UML diagrams. Let us consider the red-crossing
  1460	function that we already used to demonstrate the activity of complete-
  1461	ness check and requirements completion, and let us generate a UML
  1462	sequence diagram. When dealing with non-multimodal LLMs, i.e., mod-
  1463	els that can only generate texts and not graphical representations, it can
  1464	be useful to resort to the PlantUML structured language and the tool
  1465	PlantText.10This is a tool that interprets the language and visualises the
  1466	model, as done by Ferrari et al. [9]. Prompt: Please act as a requirements
  1467	engineer expert in system design. Generate a UML sequence diagram in
  1468	10https://www.planttext.com/ Last visit 30 Oct. 2024.
  1469
  1470	--- Page 14 ---
  1471	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
  1472	14A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
  1473	PlantUML  so that it can be visualised  with PlantText.  Requirements:  {...}.
  1474	The requirements  used are the same as those illustrated  for the red-
  1475	crossing  function  after their completion,  and the output  is depicted  in
  1476	Fig. 3.
  1477	Requirements  Classification After requirements  have been analysed,
  1478	one can imagine  classifying  them into different  nonfunctional  cat-
  1479	egories  to facilitate  structured  requirements  documentation  and re-
  1480	quirements  storage  for reuse. Requirements  classification  is one of the
  1481	most common  tasks in the NLP for RE field [31]. Prompt: Please act
  1482	as a requirements  classification  tool. What are the possible  nonfunctional
  1483	requirements  classes  of this requirement?  ‘‘The authentication  system  should
  1484	be easy to use and prevent  access from underage  users’’.
  1485	The requirement  can be classified  into the following  nonfunc-
  1486	tional requirements  classes:
  1487	1.Usability  or User Experience: This class encompasses  the
  1488	aspect  of the requirement  that focuses  on the system  being
  1489	‘‘easy to use’’. It pertains  to the user’s interaction  with the
  1490	authentication  system,  ensuring  that it is intuitive  and user-
  1491	friendly  and minimises  the cognitive  effort required  for
  1492	users to authenticate  themselves  successfully.
  1493	2.Security: This class covers  the aspect  of the requirement
  1494	that emphasises  the system’s  ability  to ‘‘prevent  access from
  1495	underage  users’’.  It deals with security-related  concerns,
  1496	including  access control  mechanisms,  user authentication
  1497	processes,  and measures  to ensure  that only authorised
  1498	users who meet certain  criteria,  like being of a certain  age,
  1499	can access the system.
  1500	It is interesting  to notice that the LLM is able to actually  identify  two
  1501	possible  classes,  which underlines  its ability  to have a clear compre-
  1502	hension  of the nuances  of the requirement  statement.  A requirements
  1503	classification  system  can be foreseen  in which one inputs a set of
  1504	possible  classes,  and the LLM produces  a less verbose  output  where each
  1505	requirement  is associated  with its class, as done in previous  work [37].
  1506	Requirements  Tracing Another  typical  activity  in mission-critical  RE
  1507	processes  is requirements  tracing,  which enables  the identification  of
  1508	trace relations  between  different  requirements  and between  require-
  1509	ments and other process  artifacts,  e.g., models,  code, and tests. Let
  1510	us consider  the problem  of requirements-to-requirements  tracing  with
  1511	LLMs: Please act as a requirements  tracing  tool. Shall these two require-
  1512	ments be traced to each other? ’’1. the onboard  system  shall have a
  1513	dashboard’’.  ’’2. the onboard  system  shall notify the driver of the maximum
  1514	speed through  an electronic  speedometer’’.
  1515	Yes, these two requirements  should  be traced  to each
  1516	other. They are highly  related  because  they belong  to the same
  1517	system  and have a common  function.  The onboard  system  is re-
  1518	sponsible  for displaying  information  to the driver and passengers,
  1519	and one of the information  is the maximum  speed. The electronic
  1520	speedometer  is a component  of the dashboard  that shows  the
  1521	maximum  speed to the driver. Therefore,  there is a dependency
  1522	and a consistency  between  these two requirements,  and they
  1523	should  be traced  to each other.
  1524	It is worth noting  that the LLM is able to infer that the speedometer
  1525	is actually  part of the dashboard,  although  this is not explicitly  stated
  1526	in the second  requirement.
  1527	Code, Comments,  Test Generation,  and other Code-related  Tasks Fi-
  1528	nally, one can go ahead with the classical  development  activities  with
  1529	code generation,  code documentation  through  comments,  and test gen-
  1530	eration.  Other activities  that could be performed  with LLMs, and not
  1531	Fig. 4.Overall view of the salient points of the roadmap  on using FMs to support
  1532	LLM-based  development.
  1533	illustrated  in this showcase  are code summarisation  [144], code revi-
  1534	sion [145]—see  Hou et al. [3] for a comprehensive  overview  of code
  1535	activities  enhanced  by LLMs. Code-centric  tasks typically  require  the
  1536	usage of LLMs specialised  for code tasks, such as CodeLlama  [113] or
  1537	StarCoder  [114]. We do not illustrate  these code-related  tasks, as our
  1538	paper is mainly  focused  on LLMs for RE.
  1539	6. Roadmap:  Using FMs to support  LLM-based  development
  1540	The LLM-based  software  development  process  exemplified  in Sec-
  1541	tion 5provides  a view of how LLMs can be exploited  to support
  1542	different  RE and software  engineering  tasks. The illustrated  workflow
  1543	can be implemented  through  LLM agents  [30], which can perform
  1544	specific  RE and SE tasks, also using external  resources,  e.g., knowledge
  1545	bases or external  libraries.  However,  by delegating  part of the system
  1546	development  to LLMs, there is a risk of losing control  of the process,  es-
  1547	pecially  when LLM agents  are automatically  orchestrated.  In our vision,
  1548	FMs can provide  the means  to restore  this control.  In the following,
  1549	we report research  avenues  in this direction,  again highlighting  action
  1550	points with the symbol
  1551	 .Fig. 4summarises  and connects  the different
  1552	research  avenues  discussed.  Each discussion  item is associated  with
  1553	a circled  number,  which appears  in the figure and in the following
  1554	paragraphs.  We will describe  the figure at the end of the section  to
  1555	provide  a comprehensive  view of the roadmap.
  1556	Ensuring  Correctness  through  Formal  Requirements  and Argumen-
  1557	tation 1As well known,  LLMs can sometimes  generate  incorrect
  1558	solutions  to RE problems  or ‘‘hallucinations’’.  These may not be imme-
  1559	diately  evident  since the output,  being it a requirement,  a specification,
  1560	or another  RE artifact,  may appear  sufficiently  plausible  to the user. We
  1561	argue that, in the field of RE, addressing  this problem  is not different
  1562	from the task of requirements  quality  assurance,  in which analysts
  1563	review  the human-written  requirements  for ambiguity,  inconsistency,
  1564	and other defects.  In principle,  quality  assurance  will need to be
  1565
  1566	--- Page 15 ---
  1567	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
  1568	15A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
  1569	applied to LLM-generated artefacts rather than to human-written ones.
  1570	However, quality assurance through manual review can be tedious and
  1571	error-prone, and NLP solutions have long been experimented with to
  1572	facilitate the work of requirements analysts, e.g., by detecting ambi-
  1573	guities, incompleteness, and other defects [31]. In a world where the
  1574	main NLP solutions for requirements analysis are based on LLMs, we
  1575	need other means to ensure requirements quality since one cannot
  1576	fully rely on their output. Moreover, given the plausibility of LLM
  1577	answers, human analysts and especially novices may tend to rely on the
  1578	LLM’s responses without analysing their truthfulness with a sufficiently
  1579	critical eye. Formal notations have been explored in the past as one
  1580	of the solutions to increase requirements quality, as they can ensure
  1581	that requirements are documented in a way that can unequivocally be
  1582	interpreted [10]. While their practical application has often been hin-
  1583	dered by their inherent complexity, the explanation potential offered
  1584	by LLMs discussed in Section 4can in turn help their introduction in
  1585	the RE quality assurance process. What if the explanation is also faulty?
  1586	The definition of formal specifications, their verification against the for-
  1587	malised requirements, and the provable consistency of the FM-centred
  1588	process can mitigate this problem.
  1589	Another important support to ensure the correctness of the out-
  1590	put can come from formal argumentation theory. Its usage has been
  1591	explored in RE to support ambiguity identification [146], and repre-
  1592	sent the conversation between requirements analysts and stakehold-
  1593	ers [147]. It has been shown that LLMs possess weak inference abilities
  1594	and still lag in terms of logical coherence [148], which leads to in-
  1595	correct output. Constraining the LLM responses to adhere to a certain
  1596	formal argumentative structure can give evidence of the soundness of
  1597	the proposed reasoning, thus mitigating hallucinations and incorrect
  1598	answers.
  1599	Action Point: To ensure the correctness of LLM-generated so-
  1600	lutions, a formal development process can be adopted, supported by
  1601	LLM-generated explanations. Moreover, the use of a formal augmenta-
  1602	tive structure to constrain LLM responses can enhance logical coherence
  1603	and mitigate hallucinations.
  1604	Improving Mathematical Reasoning with Formal LLMs 2LLMs have
  1605	demonstrated tremendous abilities when dealing with language-related
  1606	generative problems, such as text summarisation or question-answering.
  1607	However, they have also shown difficulties when dealing with mathe-
  1608	matical problems, even trivial ones. Requirements for cyber–physical
  1609	systems often include mathematical formulae [149], used to model
  1610	physical phenomena, which need to be embedded in the software,
  1611	e.g., through numerical approximation. These requirements can prove
  1612	to be challenging, especially when using LLMs to generate code. The
  1613	lack of mathematical ability of LLMs can, in principle, be due to the
  1614	lack of mathematical and logic data used during the training process.
  1615	Creating an LLM using mathematical knowledge has been experimented
  1616	with in LLMs such as Lemma [150], which has also been used to
  1617	support theorem proving, and MathStral.11However, other authors
  1618	have highlighted that there may be inherent barriers to the mathemat-
  1619	ical reasoning of LLMs, since they are not robust to small numerical
  1620	variations, or modification of logical clauses in their prompts [151].
  1621	RAG solutions can provide help in this direction by introducing external
  1622	knowledge based on formal notions that can be retrieved and used by
  1623	the LLM when addressing logical, mathematical, or formal problems,
  1624	as done, e.g., in LeanDojo [152]. Similar approaches can be followed
  1625	in RE and SE by creating RAG systems that embed FM knowledge. A
  1626	possible alternative is to create multiple LLMs based on FM knowledge,
  1627	code knowledge (e.g., CodeLlama [113], StarCoder [114]), and NL
  1628	knowledge and devise approaches to interrogate them simultaneously
  1629	and combine their output in a meaningful way. Finally, the LLM agent
  1630	paradigm [30] can be used to access external libraries and resources,
  1631	11https://huggingface.co/mistralai/Mathstral-7B-v0.1 . Last visit 21 Oct.
  1632	2024.e.g., a reasoner or a calculator.
  1633	Action Point: The challenges of using LLMs for generating code
  1634	that embeds mathematical constraints can be addressed by leverag-
  1635	ing specialised training, dynamic knowledge retrieval with RAG, and
  1636	collaboration between multiple LLM agents embedding different types
  1637	of knowledge (code, FM, and NL). The agents can also use external
  1638	resources, e.g., for computation and logic reasoning.
  1639	Formal Prompt Engineering 3With LLM, prompt engineering has
  1640	emerged as an entirely new discipline, focused on crafting the most
  1641	appropriate NL query for an LLM to solve the task at hand [153].
  1642	When prompting to generate source code or design models, prompts
  1643	basically become requirements to be fulfilled, and, in this context, RE
  1644	boils down to prompt engineering. Similar to requirements, NL prompts
  1645	can suffer from ambiguity issues, which can have consequences on
  1646	generated artefacts [9], and their expression can benefit from the usage
  1647	of formal notations or rigorous controlled NLs. In this sense, researchers
  1648	are encouraged to experiment with the usage of notations such as ACSL
  1649	(ANSI/ISO C Specification Language), which helps formally specify
  1650	the expected behaviour of C programs and is used by the FRAMA-
  1651	C environment [105]. By specifying formal pre- and post-conditions
  1652	directly in the prompts, one can have the guarantee that the constraints
  1653	are not only considered during generation but are also embedded in the
  1654	generated code and can be checked by the analysis environment.
  1655	The proposed idea can be effective when one expects to gener-
  1656	ate single functions, but it does not scale to large software systems,
  1657	as the specified conditions are typically low-level, i.e., associated
  1658	with code variables. Instead, when complex software systems need
  1659	to be generated, requirements analysts need to engineer architectures
  1660	of inter-connected and inter-dependent prompts. The design of this
  1661	architecture can benefit from classical semi-formal languages such as
  1662	UML, which allow the representation of relations between compo-
  1663	nents (i.e., prompts, in this context) and guide their constructions
  1664	and orchestration. Researchers are thus invited to adapt consolidated
  1665	model-driven development principles and approaches to the novel
  1666	scenario of prompt engineering, possibly evolving and adapting them
  1667	to account for the peculiarities of prompt and prompt relations.
  1668	Action Point: Using formal notation or controlled natural lan-
  1669	guages can help enhance the precision of NL prompts. Incorporating
  1670	formal pre- and post-conditions in prompts can help embed constraints
  1671	in generated code. For complex systems, we can develop interconnected
  1672	prompt architectures using semi-formal languages to represent relation-
  1673	ships between prompts and guide their orchestration. To this end, an
  1674	LLM agent-based paradigm [30] can be used.
  1675	Formal Domain Knowledge and Explainability 4Domain knowledge
  1676	is one of the key dimensions of any RE endeavour, as the interpretation
  1677	and analysis of the requirements first require a thorough understanding
  1678	of the domain of the system-to-be, its stakeholders, application context,
  1679	and constraints. LLMs tend to acquire knowledge from large amounts
  1680	of unstructured input documents and encapsulate it in the parameters
  1681	adjusted through the learning process. This has been possible because
  1682	of the large availability of NL documents on the web, which have been
  1683	used as a stream of information to train LLMs. However, when dealing
  1684	with domain- and project-specific knowledge, for which textual infor-
  1685	mation is scarce, LLM cannot be expected to perform as well as for tasks
  1686	requiring general knowledge only. We argue that, in contexts where
  1687	domain-specific corpora are limited, knowledge structured through for-
  1688	mal ontologies in the form of knowledge graphs shall be injected in an
  1689	LLM-based RE process, e.g., with the support of RAG approaches. This
  1690	would address the need for abstraction that is required for reasoning
  1691	and that LLMs have demonstrated to lack in some cases [154]. As LLMs
  1692	are trained only on text, they also lack the concept of aworld model ,
  1693	i.e., ‘‘structure-preserving, behaviourally efficacious representations of
  1694	the entities and processes in the real world’’ [148,155]. Although
  1695	multi-modal LLMs are being developed [156], which can potentially
  1696	address this issue, formal representations of the entities and processes
  1697	in the real world can address three important problems. First, formal
  1698
