     9	Formal requirements engineering and large language models: A two-way
    10	roadmap
    11	Alessio Ferraria,b
    12	,∗,Paola Spoletinic
    13	aConsiglio Nazionale delle Ricerche (CNR), Istituto di Scienza e Tecnologie dell’Informazione "A. Faedo" (ISTI), Pisa, Italy
    14	bUniversity College Dublin (UCD), School of Computer Science, Dublin, Ireland
    15	cKennesaw State University, GA, USA
    16	A R T I C L E I N F O
    17	Keywords:
    18	Requirements engineering
    19	Formal methods
    20	Large language models
    21	LLMs
    22	Natural language processing
    23	NLP
    24	NLP4RE
    25	Prompt engineering
    26	Prompt requirements engineeringA B S T R A C T
    27	Context: Large Language Models (LLMs) have made remarkable advancements in emulating human linguistic
    28	capabilities, showing potential also in executing various requirements engineering (RE) tasks. However, despite
    29	their generally good performance, the adoption of LLM-generated solutions and artefacts prompts concerns
    30	about their correctness, fairness, and trustworthiness.
    31	Objective: This paper aims to address the concerns associated with the use of LLMs in RE activities.
    32	Specifically, it seeks to develop a roadmap that leverages formal methods (FMs) to provide guarantees of
    33	correctness, fairness, and trustworthiness when LLMs are utilised in RE. Symmetrically, it aims to explore how
    34	LLMs can be employed to make FMs more accessible.
    35	Methods: We use two sets of examples to show the current limits of FMs when used in software development
    36	and of LLMs when used for RE tasks. The highlighted limitations are addressed by proposing two roadmaps
    37	grounded in the current literature and technologies.
    38	Results: The proposed examples show the potential and limits of FMs in supporting software development
    39	and of LLMs when used for RE tasks. The initial investigation into how these limitations can be overcome has
    40	been concretised in two detailed roadmaps for the RE and, more largely, the software engineering community.
    41	Conclusion: The proposed roadmaps offer a promising approach to address the concerns of correctness,
    42	fairness, and trustworthiness associated with the use of LLMs in RE tasks through the use of FMs and to
    43	enhance the accessibility of FMs by utilising LLMs.
    44	1. Introduction
    45	Large Language  Models  (LLMs)  [1] have shown  remarkable  skill
    46	in mimicking  human  linguistic  abilities.  These models  are capable  of
    47	processing  and generating  human-like  text based on learned  patterns,
    48	which has led to their widespread  application  in numerous  fields,
    49	including  software  engineering  (SE). In SE, LLMs contribute  to various
    50	activities  such as code synthesis,  documentation  creation,  and ideation,
    51	thereby  improving  the efficiency  of processes  that traditionally  re-
    52	quire extensive  human  effort [2,3]. Despite  slower  initial adoption
    53	in requirements  engineering  (RE) compared  to other SE fields, recent
    54	literature  demonstrates  significant  potential  for LLMs to enhance  ef-
    55	ficiency  and tackle traditional  challenges  [3]. Recent  growth  in LLM
    56	research  within  RE includes  summarisation  of requirements  [4], user
    57	story generation  [5], traceability  support  through  advanced  prompting
    58	strategies  [6] and model generation  [7–9]. While these approaches
    59	∗Corresponding  author.
    60	E-mail addresses: alessio.ferrari@isti.cnr.it (A. Ferrari) ,pspoleti@kennesaw.edu (P. Spoletini) .deliver  promising  results,  questions  arise about the reliability,  correct-
    61	ness, and interpretability  of the results  generated  by these models.  To
    62	address  these concerns,  we propose  using formal  requirements  and for-
    63	mal verification  techniques  to provide  guarantees  on the LLM-assisted
    64	activities.
    65	Formal  requirements  provide  a structured  and clear specification  of
    66	a system’s  expected  behaviour  and constraints  [10]. They precisely  out-
    67	line system  functionalities  and qualities.  Unlike  informal  requirements,
    68	which can be ambiguous,  formal  requirements  use mathematical  or
    69	logical  language  to minimise  the risk of misinterpretation.  The adoption
    70	of formal  requirements  enables  the use of formal  methods  (FMs),  which
    71	are mathematical  techniques  for analysing  and verifying  systems.  These
    72	methods,  such as model checking  [11], abstract  interpretation  [12],
    73	and deductive  verification  [13], aim to increase  the rigour and cor-
    74	rectness  of the software  development  process.  However,  due to the
    75	specialised  knowledge  required  to apply formal  requirements  and their
    76	https://doi.org/10.1016/j.infsof.2025.107697
    77	Received  10 July 2024; Received  in revised  form 6 February  2025; Accepted  7 February  2025
    78
    79	--- Page 2 ---
    80	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
    81	2A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
    82	associated verification techniques, their use is predominantly confined
    83	to the development of critical and complex systems.
    84	Given the potential central role of formal approaches in software
    85	development, especially for complex and critical systems, our vision
    86	also includes steps to make formal RE more accessible. To this end,
    87	we propose to use LLMs to support the formalisation of requirements,
    88	explain formal requirements, and help interpret the results of formal
    89	tools.
    90	In this paper, we extend our initial vision presented in [14] by
    91	providing the following contributions:
    92	•An overview of LLMs and their applications in RE, along with a
    93	discussion on formal RE1(Section 2);
    94	•A comprehensive roadmap to use LLMs to make them more usable
    95	in practice (Section 4) supported by a simple example of the use
    96	of formal RE in software development (Section 3);
    97	•A detailed roadmap to use formal RE to overcome the limitations
    98	of LLMs in supporting RE activities (Section 6), motivated by
    99	a set of examples illustrating the use of LLMs in the software
   100	engineering process (Section 5); and
   101	•Considerations on the practical implementation of the roadmaps,
   102	with associated risks and limitations (Section 7).
   103	It is worth remarking that this is a vision paper, which does not
   104	aim to offer sound empirical evidence but rather to indicate possi-
   105	ble avenues of research. Nevertheless, we have made an effort to
   106	make the vision practical by pointing to seminal works that can shape
   107	future research and by including practical considerations as well as
   108	potential barriers to realising the vision. We also want to emphasise
   109	that, despite our efforts to touch upon multiple relevant points, the
   110	discussed roadmaps should not be considered exhaustive. They reflect
   111	the opinions and experience of the authors, who have a background
   112	in RE, FMs, and natural language processing (NLP). Moreover, it is
   113	designed to encourage further reflection, with the potential to either
   114	expand upon its content or challenge the plausibility and relevance of
   115	certain research directions.
   116	2. Background
   117	In this section, we provide an overview of the field of LLMs, also
   118	including applications to the RE field, and some background on formal
   119	RE.
   120	2.1. Large language models and requirements engineering
   121	A language model (LM) is a computational model designed to
   122	represent human language by capturing the statistical structure and
   123	semantic relationships within a corpus of textual data. The construction
   124	of an LM encompasses various techniques and methodologies that have
   125	evolved over time. In the following, we present a brief history of LMs,
   126	followed by a discussion of studies using LMs in RE.
   127	Traditional language models. The earliest LMs, such as bag-of-words
   128	(BoW) and term frequency-inverse document frequency (tf-idf), focus
   129	on representing textual documents based on the frequency of individual
   130	words [15]. Given a document,2BoW disregards the order of words
   131	in the document and treats it as an unordered collection of words.
   132	1With the term formal RE, we characterised all those mathematical-based
   133	techniques that specify, model, and verify software requirements, such as
   134	techniques to formally express properties and describe software models and
   135	tools for verification.
   136	2The term ‘‘document’’ here has the general meaning of ‘‘finite sequence
   137	of tokens representing a single conceptual entity’’, where tokens are words,
   138	punctuation, spaces, numbers, etc. In other terms, a document can also be a
   139	sentence, paragraph, query, Tweet, or any individual unit of text that conveys
   140	a coherent message or idea.This leads to a vector representation for documents, in which each
   141	component of the vector is associated with a word, and the content
   142	of the component is the frequency of occurrence of the word in the
   143	document. Instead, tf-idf emphasises theimportance of words in a
   144	document relative to their occurrence across a corpus of documents.
   145	The frequency of a word is weighted based on its frequency in the
   146	corpus, with the assumption that a word that occurs more frequently is
   147	less informative (i.e., less characterising for the document) than a word
   148	that occurs less frequently. Given a document, this is again represented
   149	as a vector where each word is a component, but the content of the
   150	component is the word frequency divided by the frequency of the word
   151	in the corpus. These traditional LMs are typically used to compare their
   152	similarity for the purpose of information retrieval or clustering. The
   153	similarity is typically computed in the vector space induced by the
   154	vectorial representation of documents, typically by means of thecosine
   155	similarity [15]. The closer the cosine between the vectors is to one, the
   156	more similar are the compared documents.
   157	Word embeddings. Traditional LMs suffer from the lack of represen-
   158	tation of themeaning of the words that they were representing. In
   159	the vector spaces induced by these representations, sentences using
   160	different words but having similar meanings would not be considered
   161	similar to each other, as their vectors would be completely different.
   162	To solve this problem, word embeddings were introduced in 2013 by
   163	Mikolov et al. [16], following the distributional semantics idea that
   164	the meaning of a word is determined ‘‘by the company it keeps’’ [17],
   165	which implies that words derive their meaning from the context in
   166	which they are used rather than from abstract definitions. According to
   167	this vision, each word is represented as a dense vector in a continuous
   168	vector space. In this vector space, words that have a strong association,
   169	because they typically occur in similar textual contexts (i.e., together
   170	with the same words), are represented through vectors that are closer
   171	to each others. These vectors are built through a shallow feedforward
   172	neural network trained on a large textual corpus, with a task that
   173	enables the network to learn the typical contexts where words occur.
   174	To this end, the learning of word embeddings LM is based on a training
   175	task where the objective is to predict surrounding words given a target
   176	word or vice versa. word2vec [16] is one of the first algorithms
   177	for word embeddings, and was followed by GloVe, FasText, Universal
   178	Sentence Encoder (USE), and others—see Johnson [18] for a summary.
   179	Once words are represented with these types of LMs, their represen-
   180	tation can be used to perform other downstream tasks, e.g., sentence
   181	classification, language translation, etc., typically using the vectors as
   182	input to train task-specific machine learning algorithms.
   183	Pre-trained transformers and BERT. In word embeddings, each word
   184	is represented as a fixed, dense vector in a continuous vector space
   185	based solely on its co-occurrence statistics within a large corpus of
   186	text. These embeddings capture semantic similarities between words
   187	based on their distributional patterns in the training data. However,
   188	word embeddings lack contextual information about how a word’s
   189	meaning might change depending on its surrounding words in a spe-
   190	cific sentence or context. As a result, words with multiple meanings
   191	or usages may have a single representation, leading to ambiguity in
   192	capturing the nuanced meaning of words in different contexts. To solve
   193	this issue, the Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers
   194	(BERT) architecture was introduced [19]. BERT employs a transformer
   195	architecture, which is a deep neural network specifically designed for
   196	sequential data processing. Using this type of architecture in NLP tasks
   197	typically consists of two phases: pre-training , i.e., learning an LM from
   198	a document corpus, and fine-tuning , i.e., further training the learned
   199	LM on a specific downstream task, such as classification or information
   200	extraction, with task-specific annotated data.
   201	During pre-training, BERT learns contextualised representations of
   202	words from a corpus by considering the entire input sentence bidirec-
   203	tionally. This means that each word’s representation is dynamically
   204	influenced by its surrounding words in the sentence, allowing BERT
   205
   206	--- Page 3 ---
   207	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
   208	3A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
   209	to capture intricate semantic relationships and contextual nuances.
   210	BERT achieves this bidirectional contextual understanding through a
   211	process called masked language modelling . During pre-training, BERT
   212	randomly masks some of the input words in a sentence and tasks the
   213	model with predicting the masked words based on the surrounding
   214	context. Furthermore, BERT incorporates the concept ofself-attention
   215	mechanisms, which allows it to weigh the importance of different
   216	words in a sentence dynamically based on their contextual relevance.
   217	This attention mechanism enables BERT to focus more on informative
   218	words while disregarding irrelevant ones, leading to richer and more
   219	contextually relevant representations of text. After pre-training, BERT-
   220	based word representation learned from an input corpus can be used,
   221	e.g., to encode sentences. The encoding of a word will then vary
   222	based on its context in the input sentence—i.e., surrounding words and
   223	syntactic structure.
   224	Pre-trained BERT models are then fine-tuned on specific down-
   225	stream tasks, such as text classification, named entity recognition, and
   226	question answering, by adding task-specific layers on top of the pre-
   227	trained BERT architecture. This fine-tuning process adapts the learned
   228	word representations to the specific characteristics of the target task.
   229	Fine-tuning can be performed also on domain-specific documents, so
   230	that, instead of learning a task, the LM can improve its understanding of
   231	the language of the domain. Based on the BERT architecture, different
   232	alternatives have been proposed, such as RoBERTa (Robustly optimised
   233	BERT approach), XLNet, Electra (Efficiently Learning an Encoder that
   234	Classifies Token Replacements Accurately), and others—see Kalyan
   235	et al. [20] for a survey.
   236	Large language models (LLMs). The transformer-based architecture of
   237	BERT is also used in today’s LLMs, such as GPT, Llama, Mixtral, Gemini,
   238	and others—see Minaee et al. for a survey [21]. LLMs are typically
   239	larger in terms of the number of parameters (e.g., weights and biases in
   240	the neural network) and natural network layers compared to BERT and
   241	previous transformers. These larger models require more computational
   242	resources for training and inference. For example, while BERT in its
   243	base version has around 110 million parameters, it is estimated that
   244	GPT-4 could have around 100 trillion parameters. LLMs are designed
   245	to excel across a wide range of NLP tasks without extensive task-
   246	specific fine-tuning, which is typically required for BERT-like LMs.
   247	LLMs demonstrate impressive capabilities in downstream tasks without
   248	examples (zero-shot learning) or with few examples of the task input
   249	and output (few-shot learning). LLMs are frequently based on the
   250	question-answering paradigm, where the downstream task is expressed
   251	with aprompt , i.e., a natural language (NL) query or instruction,
   252	which the LLM is required to answer or perform. The introduction
   253	of this paradigm has led to the development of a large variety of
   254	prompt strategies, such as chain of thought (CoT) prompting [22],
   255	generated knowledge prompting [23], see Quiao et al. [24] for a survey.
   256	Further techniques for exploiting the capabilities of LLMs, and espe-
   257	cially avoiding costly re-training operations, include instruction fine-
   258	tuning [25], retrieval augmented generation (RAG) [26], Low-Rank
   259	Adaptation (LoRA) [27], and distillation [28].
   260	Instruction fine-tuning, or simply instruction tuning, aims to im-
   261	prove the LLM’s ability to follow instructions expressed in NL and
   262	consists of fine-tuning the LLM to perform instruction-based tasks. In
   263	the more classical LM fine-tuning, the training dataset typically consists
   264	of a data item with associated annotations (e.g., a requirement and its
   265	class). With instruction tuning, the training dataset consists of a NL
   266	instruction, which specifies the task to be addressed (e.g., what is the
   267	class of the following requirement?) and the expected answer (e.g., the
   268	actual class). This has been shown to improve the capability of the LLM
   269	to follow specific instructions [29].
   270	RAG enhances the capability of LLMs to produce accurate and
   271	context-relevant output by incorporating external information in the
   272	generation process. When tasked with a certain prompt, prompt-relevant
   273	information is first retrieved from an external knowledge source. Theoriginal prompt is enhanced with the additional information, and the
   274	LLM is queried with this prompt to produce the desired output. This
   275	enables the possibility to combine the language understanding of LLM
   276	with external, typically domain-specific knowledge.
   277	LoRA is a technique used to adapt large pre-trained language models
   278	to specific tasks or domains without retraining all the model param-
   279	eters. LoRA starts by freezing the weight matrices of the pre-trained
   280	LLM, which means these weights are not updated during the adapta-
   281	tion process. LoRA introduces additional matrices into the model that
   282	are smaller in size compared to the model’s original weights. These
   283	matrices are trainable, meaning that they can be adjusted during the
   284	fine-tuning process based on the task-specific data. The fine-tuning does
   285	not modify the weights of the original LM, while modifies the output
   286	of the large matrices during the forward pass of the neural network.
   287	Finally, distillation, or knowledge distillation, is a technique aimed
   288	at transferring the knowledge and capabilities from a large, complex
   289	model (often referred to as theteacher ) to a smaller, more efficient
   290	model (known as thestudent ). The goal is to enable the student model
   291	to perform as closely as possible to the teacher model while being less
   292	resource-intensive.
   293	Another relevant issue to address is the complexity of certain tasks,
   294	which cannot be addressed with a single LLM, but require multiple
   295	LLMs or external resources. To cope with this problem, LLM agents
   296	have emerged [30]. An LLM agent is a system that not only generates
   297	human-like text but also has additional abilities to handle sophisticated,
   298	multi-step workflows, perform reasoning, remember information, and
   299	use tools or external systems, e.g., through APIs, to complete specific
   300	tasks. In a system based on LLM agents, a main LLM acts as the central
   301	controller or ‘‘brain’’, orchestrating a sequence of actions required to
   302	fulfil a task or user request, leveraging other LLM agents. LLM agents
   303	can decide when to use certain modules (e.g., retrieving stored knowl-
   304	edge or performing numerical calculations) and adjust their approach
   305	based on the task at hand. Frameworks supporting the development of
   306	LLM agents are LangChain.3and AutoGPT.4
   307	LMs and LLMs in RE. NLP techniques in general and LMs, in particular,
   308	have been widely studied and applied in RE since its early days—
   309	the first works appeared already in the ’80s [31]. Typical RE tasks
   310	that have been addressed with the support of LMs are ambiguity and
   311	smell detection [32], tracing [33], classification of requirements [34],
   312	as well as app reviews [35] and issues [36], and other tasks. Early
   313	studies using tf-idf and BoW focused on solutions for tracing and
   314	for requirements retrieval from large repositories. In these studies,
   315	these prototypical LMs were mainly exploited to measure the similarity
   316	between requirements so that trace links could be identified and re-
   317	quirements could be retrieved from existing repositories with the goal
   318	of requirements reuse. Word embeddings were also used, specifically
   319	for the identification of cross-domain ambiguity, i.e., terms that have
   320	different meanings in different domains, and to support requirements
   321	tracing with the aid of recurrent neural networks [33]. BERT-like trans-
   322	formers were first exploited for requirements classification [34,37,38],
   323	and saw applications in a multitude of tasks, including requirements re-
   324	trieval [39], app review analysis [40], and assessment of requirements
   325	completeness [41].
   326	The uptake of LLM-based techniques has been rather slow in RE
   327	compared to other SE subfields [3], although some exploratory work
   328	has been started. Let us consider some relevant contributions. Among
   329	the early works, Jain et al. [4] presents a set of experiments on the us-
   330	age of different LLMs for requirements summarisation from legal texts.
   331	They first generated summaries with the aid of GPT-3 and used them
   332	to fine-tune Pegasus, GPT-2, and other open-source models that do not
   333	require the disclosure of data, as in the case of GPT-3. Then, they eval-
   334	uated the performance of these models on industrial datasets, showing
   335	3https://www.langchain.com/ Last visit 30 Oct., 2024.
   336	4https://autogpt.net/category/autogpt/ . Last visit 30 Oct., 2024.
   337
   338	--- Page 4 ---
   339	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
   340	4A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
   341	that Pegasus outperforms the other options. Marczak et al. [5] use Chat-
   342	GPT to generate user stories reflecting human values. The user stories
   343	are then used as creativity triggers in requirements elicitation sessions.
   344	The evaluation with focus groups shows that the generated user stories
   345	effectively inspire participants to identify value-relevant requirements.
   346	Rodriguez et al. [6] experiment with prompting strategies to support
   347	requirements traceability with the aid of Anthropic’s Claude instant
   348	model. Their exploratory evaluation with the CM1 dataset shows that
   349	small prompt modifications can lead to significant differences in model
   350	outputs.
   351	Some works also explore the potential of LLMs for model generation.
   352	Among them, Chen et al. [7] evaluates the potential of GPT-4 for
   353	generating goal models using the textual grammar for the Goal-oriented
   354	Requirement Language (GRL) based on free-form NL descriptions of the
   355	problem context. Their experiments on four example cases show that
   356	incremental prompts can improve the results, and domain knowledge
   357	is highly needed to assess the plausible—yet frequently incorrect—
   358	output. Chen et al. [42] evaluates GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 for generating
   359	class diagrams based on NL descriptions. Their evaluation on a ground
   360	truth from an undergraduate course shows that the LLMs struggle to
   361	generate relationships between classes. Furthermore, they show that
   362	while few-shot approaches (i.e., adding examples to the prompts) can
   363	improve performance, chain-of-thought prompting (i.e., adding rea-
   364	soning steps) leads to a performance decrease. Still on class diagram
   365	generation, Camára et al. [8] perform an exploratory study using
   366	ChatGPT. They exercise the model with different sample problems
   367	and provide some lessons learned. The authors conclude that iterative
   368	prompting is needed to produce models of sufficient quality, and adher-
   369	ence to the standard in terms of construct semantics tends to be poor.
   370	Finally, the study of Ferrari et al. [9] explores the capability of ChatGPT
   371	to generate UML sequence diagrams from requirements. The authors
   372	observed that, although the models generally conform to the standard
   373	and exhibit a reasonable level of understandability, their correctness
   374	with respect to the specified requirements often presents challenges.
   375	This issue is particularly pronounced in the presence of requirements
   376	smells, such as ambiguity and inconsistency.
   377	Besides these initial efforts, recent months have seen substantial
   378	growth in the literature in the area of LLMs for RE, as is evident
   379	from the accepted papers at RE@Next! 2024, which includes seven
   380	papers about LLMs out of 19 contributions, and at RE 2024, which
   381	includes eight papers out of 14 contributions.5These papers cover a
   382	variety of RE tasks through the use of LLMs, from an approach that
   383	uses GPT for automating text completion of requirements within the
   384	context of the specification [43] to the use of in-context learning to
   385	support the evaluation of satisfaction arguments, which describe how
   386	a requirement is satisfied by a system specification [44]. Concerning
   387	the task of requirements elicitation, Gorer et al. propose an approach
   388	that uses LLMs to generate interview scripts [45]. The use of LLMs is
   389	also proposed to elicit and specify SRS documents [46] and check the
   390	quality of requirements [47]. Additional exploratory studies investigate
   391	the use of LLMs to generate code from requirements [48] or to improve
   392	the representation of normative requirements [49].
   393	2.2. Formal methods and formal RE
   394	When formal techniques are applied, the system requirements are
   395	represented through a formal specification that defines ‘‘what’’ the
   396	system should do and the constraints it must operate under (e.g., [50]).
   397	Then, either this specification is directly analysed, or the system is
   398	modelled through a state-based formalism, which describes ‘‘how’’
   399	the system operates (e.g., [51]) and then analysed against properties
   400	extracted from the specification and often represented in mathematical
   401	logic (e.g., [52]). In the following, we will present some of the most
   402	developed and used formal techniques to specify requirements, create
   403	models, and analyse them.
   404	5https://conf.researchr.org/home/re-2024 . Last Visit 29 Oct. 2024.Formal representation of specifications. FMs for requirements specifica-
   405	tion were first used in the 1970s to cope with the need for more
   406	rigorous and precise software development methods, given the increas-
   407	ing complexity and critical use of software systems in fields such as
   408	aerospace, medical, and military domains. In addition, high-profile
   409	software failures (e.g., the partial meltdown of the Three Mile Island
   410	nuclear power plant in Pennsylvania [53]) highlighted the limitations
   411	of conventional development techniques and the need for more precise
   412	and verifiable approaches to software development.
   413	An example of formal languages for specifications is theZ Nota-
   414	tion[54,55], which uses set theory and first-order predicate logic to
   415	specify system properties and behaviours. The core construct in the Z
   416	notation is the schema, which groups related state variables and their
   417	invariants together. Schemas can be used to describe both the state of
   418	a system and the operations that can be performed on that state. They
   419	can also be composed to support modularity, allowing complex systems
   420	to be described in a structured and hierarchical manner.
   421	Analogously to the Z notation, theVienna Development Model (VDM)
   422	[56] provides a model-based approach that simplifies the design and
   423	verification of complex systems. VDM uses formal specification lan-
   424	guages based on mathematical notation to describe the system’s func-
   425	tionality, structure, and constraints. The most commonly used speci-
   426	fication languages in VDM are VDM Specification Language [57] and
   427	VDM++ [58], an object-oriented extension of VDM-SL. VDM crates
   428	mathematical models as blueprints for the system, capturing its es-
   429	sential properties and behaviours. VDM emphasises the use of formal
   430	proofs to verify that the system’s implementation satisfies its specifica-
   431	tions. Proof obligations are generated to demonstrate that the system is
   432	correct with the support of tools like VDMTools [59] and Overture [60].
   433	Differently from the Z notation and VDM, which primarily focus
   434	on providing a formal specification language, theB-Method [61] is a
   435	comprehensive formal method covering specification, refinement, and
   436	implementation with a strong emphasis on proofs and tool support.
   437	The core concepts in the B-Method are abstract machines, which rep-
   438	resent components or modules of a system, define the state of the
   439	system through variables, and specify operations that can change this
   440	state. The operations are specified through preconditions that must
   441	be true before an operation can be executed and postconditions that
   442	describe the state after the operation has been executed. The B-Method
   443	allows transforming an abstract specification into a more concrete one,
   444	step by step, until an executable implementation is achieved through
   445	subsequent refinements.
   446	In addition to these approaches, over the last three decades, a
   447	variety of languages have been introduced. Process algebras such as CCS
   448	(Calculus of Communicating Systems) [62] and CSP (Communicating
   449	Sequential Processes) [63] are formal languages used to describe and
   450	analyse the behaviour of concurrent systems. The Specification and
   451	Description Language (SDL) [64] is another formal language specifically
   452	designed for describing complex, event-driven, real-time systems, com-
   453	monly used in telecommunications and other industries. The Common
   454	Algebraic Specification Language (CASL) [65] andLanguage of Temporal
   455	Ordering Specification (LOTOS) [66], including its extended version E-
   456	LOTOS, are algebraic and process-based languages used for specifying
   457	and verifying concurrent and distributed systems. Temporal Logic of Ac-
   458	tions (TLA+) [67] andAlloy [68] are used for specifying and modelling
   459	systems, with Alloy focusing on relational logic and TLA+ on temporal
   460	logic. Finally, some specification languages are inspired by program-
   461	ming languages or natural languages. For example, FizzBee [69] is
   462	a Python-inspired design specification language. Attempto Controlled
   463	English (ACE) [70] is a controlled natural language that allows spec-
   464	ifications to be written in a subset of English, aiming to reduce the
   465	complexity of formal methods for non-expert users.
   466
   467	--- Page 5 ---
   468	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
   469	5A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
   470	Representing the properties of the system. The properties that a specifica-
   471	tion has to satisfy (e.g., safety or liveness) are usually represented using
   472	some (temporal) extension of propositional or predicate logic [71].
   473	Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) [72] is an extension of propositional logic,
   474	widely used for specifying properties over linear sequences of states.
   475	LTL is particularly effective for expressing safety and liveness prop-
   476	erties, which describe what must or must not happen during system
   477	execution. The syntax of LTL includes temporal operators such as X
   478	(next), F or⟨⟩(eventually), G or [] (globally), and U (until). Compu-
   479	tation Tree Logic (CTL) [73] is another temporal logic used to specify
   480	properties over branching structures of states, which allows for the
   481	expression of properties concerning possible future states. It combines
   482	path quantifiers A (for all paths) and E (there exists a path) with the
   483	same temporal operators used in LTL. In CTL, path quantifiers cannot
   484	be used independently from temporal operators, and this makes LTL’s
   485	and CTL’s expressiveness incomparable. Instead, CTL∗[74] combines
   486	both LTL and CTL, allowing more complex specifications by enabling
   487	both path quantifiers and temporal operators to be mixed freely. 𝜇-
   488	calculus [75] is another powerful formalism that extends temporal
   489	logics like LTL and CTL by introducing fixed-point operators. This
   490	makes it one of the most expressive logics for specifying properties of
   491	transition systems, enabling the expression of complex properties like
   492	those involving recursion.
   493	Higher-order logic (HOL) [76] extends predicate logic by allowing
   494	quantification over predicates and functions, providing a more ex-
   495	pressive framework for specifying and verifying complex properties.
   496	Additional logical formalisms used to specify properties include Modal
   497	Logic [77],Metric (MTL) and Real-Time Temporal Logic (RTTL) [78],
   498	and Probabilistic Temporal Logics (e.g., Probabilistic Computation Tree
   499	Logic [79]). Modal Logic includes modalities such as necessity and
   500	possibility to express properties about different modes of truth, such
   501	as ‘‘necessarily true’’ or ‘‘possibly true’’. This logic is useful in systems
   502	where different states or conditions can coexist, and properties need to
   503	be expressed concerning these modes. MTL and RTTL extend LTL with
   504	timing constraints, allowing the specification of properties in real-time
   505	systems. Probabilistic Temporal Logics are used to specify properties
   506	of probabilistic systems, incorporating probabilities into the logical
   507	framework.
   508	Formal models. Labelled Transition Systems (LTSs) [79] are widely used
   509	to model the behaviour of concurrent systems. An LTS consists of
   510	states and transitions between these states, where each transition is
   511	labelled with an action. Each state represents a configuration of the
   512	system, and the transition shows how to move from one configuration
   513	to another. Analogously, Finite State Machines (FSMs) [73] are used to
   514	model systems with a finite number of states and transitions between
   515	those states. The main difference between the two models is that
   516	FSMs use input symbols to determine state transitions, while LTSs
   517	use labelled actions that can represent a broader range of events or
   518	interactions. In addition, LTSs are well-suited for modelling concurrent
   519	systems and their interactions, whereas FSMs are more straightforward
   520	and often used for sequential logic. A particularly used type of FSMs
   521	isBüchi Automata [51,80], used for modelling and verifying properties
   522	of infinite sequences. These automata, as FSMs, have a finite number
   523	of states, but thanks to their acceptance condition, they can describe
   524	infinite behaviours.
   525	FSMs can also be extended to include time and probability. Timed
   526	Automata (TAs) [78] extend FSMs with clocks, allowing the modelling
   527	of real-time systems. The clock variables are used to measure the
   528	passage of time and to set guards on the transitions. TAs enable the
   529	specification of timing requirements such as deadlines, periodicity, and
   530	delays. Probabilistic and Stochastic State Machines extend the classical
   531	FSMs by incorporating probabilistic and stochastic elements. These ex-
   532	tensions enable modelling systems that exhibit random or probabilistic
   533	behaviour, which is crucial when systems operate under uncertainty.
   534	Probabilistic State Machines [81] introduce probabilities into the statetransition mechanism by regulating transitions between states using
   535	predefined probabilities. Differently, Stochastic State Machines [82]
   536	incorporate timing aspects into the probabilistic state transitions, often
   537	using stochastic processes like Markov chains.
   538	The need to describe the hierarchical structure of software has
   539	led to the introduction of different hierarchical models, such asStat-
   540	echarts [83]. Statecharts extend FSMs by incorporating concepts of
   541	hierarchy, concurrency, and communication. They allow the definition
   542	of hierarchical states that are refined by substates to capture different
   543	levels of abstraction. In addition, they model concurrency through
   544	parallel states. Other hierarchical models include Hierarchical State
   545	Machines [84],MODELICA State Machines [85], and Ptolemy II State
   546	Machines [86].
   547	A commonly used tool to model a system isPetri Nets (PNs) [87],
   548	a graphical and mathematical modelling tool used to describe the flow
   549	of information and control in systems, particularly in concurrent and
   550	distributed systems. PNs used places to represent conditions or states
   551	of the system and transitions to represent events causing changes in
   552	conditions. To show that a condition is met (or the system is in a
   553	state), they use tokens that allow transitions to be executed. Analo-
   554	gously to FSMs, PNs have been extended with different attributes to
   555	make them more suitable to represent complex systems. For example,
   556	Colored PNs[88] introduce colours to tokens, enabling the modelling
   557	of systems with different types of resources; Timed PNs[89] incorpo-
   558	rate timing information into PNs, allowing transitions to have firing
   559	delays; Stochastic PNs[90] extend PNs with the probabilistic firing of
   560	transitions, incorporating uncertainty into the model; andHierarchical
   561	PNs[91] introduce hierarchy into PNs by allowing places or transitions
   562	to contain subnets.
   563	Formal methods for analysing models. There exists a variety of methods
   564	to analyse and verify the formalisms presented in this section or directly
   565	code. Given the expressive power of such formalisms and the complex-
   566	ity of the verification problem, each technique has different advantages
   567	and limitations as they need to compromise between the expressivity
   568	of the input languages and automation.
   569	Abstract Interpretation [92] uses the idea that the verification of
   570	the considered system can be done at a high level of abstraction,
   571	ignoring irrelevant details. Techniques such as interval analysis and
   572	polyhedral analysis are used to approximate the behaviour of the
   573	system. Astrèe [93] and Infer [94] are examples of tools that leverage
   574	abstract interpretation to detect potential runtime errors, such as buffer
   575	overflows and division by zero, in critical software systems.
   576	Semantic Static Analysis [95] offers a complete and automatic anal-
   577	ysis of a program’s source code without running it by using data
   578	flow analysis, control flow analysis, and type checking. This approach
   579	is supported by tools such as Coverity [96], PVS-Studio [97], and
   580	FindBugs [98] that help detect software defects and vulnerabilities and
   581	ensure compliance with coding standards by analysing source code for
   582	potential issues.
   583	Model Checking [73] verifies specifications on different types of
   584	models of the system by complete exploration. The most commonly
   585	used approaches are state space exploration and symbolic model check-
   586	ing. Spin [99] is an example of an automata-based model checker that
   587	takes as input a system modelled as a Büchi Automaton (described
   588	in PROMELA, Spin input language) and a property expressed in LTL.
   589	NuSMV [100] (and its evolution nuXmv) is a symbolic model checker
   590	for the formal verification of finite-state systems, expressed in LTS,
   591	against properties specified in CTL. UPPAAL [101], instead, is used for
   592	modelling, simulation, and verification of real-time systems.
   593	Proof Assistants are interactive tools that allow users to write proofs
   594	of theorems and are used both in mathematics and computer sci-
   595	ence. Techniques include inductive proof, coinductive proof, and auto-
   596	mated theorem proving. Tools like Coq [102], Isabelle/HOL [103], and
   597	Agda [104] assist in developing formal proofs by providing a frame-
   598	work for constructing and checking proofs and have been used in veri-
   599	fying software correctness, cryptographic protocols, and mathematical
   600
   601	--- Page 6 ---
   602	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
   603	6A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
   604	theorems.
   605	Deductive Verification [13] involves providing a program and a
   606	formal specification, which is then propagated through the source code
   607	of the program, using the weakest preconditions and symbolic execu-
   608	tion. Techniques include Hoare logic, weakest precondition calculus,
   609	and predicate transformers. Examples of tools that support deductive
   610	verification are Frama-C [105], a platform dedicated to the analysis of
   611	the source code of software written in C, Dafny [106], a verification-
   612	aware programming language that allows specifications to be written
   613	in line with the code, and KeYKeY [107], a formal verification tool for
   614	Java programs.
   615	Design by Refinement [108] is an approach based on successive
   616	refinements of a sequence of models, starting with an abstract model
   617	and ending with a concrete implementation. Techniques include step-
   618	wise refinement, data refinement, and operation refinement. Tools like
   619	Event-B [109] and Rodin, support this process by ensuring that each
   620	refinement step maintains the correctness of the system with respect to
   621	its specification. Event-B is a formal method for system-level modelling
   622	and analysis. Rodin is an open toolset for modelling and reasoning in
   623	Event-B.
   624	3. Formal software development: an example
   625	This section provides an overview of a possible software develop-
   626	ment process that includes formal models and tools. We start with
   627	the definition of requirements, the definition of a formal model, and
   628	the formalisation of the properties the model has to verify; then, we
   629	verify the model against the requirements through model checking and
   630	implement it. This is just a possible way of including formal RE in the
   631	development process. A different approach could actually start from the
   632	development of the code and then extract a formal model to be verified.
   633	In addition, one could use additional means, e.g., abstract interpreta-
   634	tion/static analysis, that can be directly applied to the verification of
   635	certain properties that are specific to the code, such as, e.g., buffer
   636	overflow, or possible NULL pointers—in case languages such as C are
   637	used.
   638	Let us consider the following requirements for a simple sender-
   639	receiver handshaking protocol that we use to guide the definition of
   640	a model and associated code:
   641	1.Message Integrity: Messages sent over the protocol must
   642	arrive intact and unaltered at the receiver’s end. Any tam-
   643	pering or corruption of the message during transmission
   644	should be detected and rejected.
   645	2.Order Preservation: Messages should be delivered to the
   646	receiver in the same order in which they were sent by the
   647	sender. Ensuring that messages are received sequentially
   648	maintains the integrity of the communication flow.
   649	3.Flow Control: The protocol should include mechanisms
   650	for flow control to prevent the sender from overwhelming
   651	the receiver with a large volume of messages. This could
   652	involve acknowledgements from the receiver to indicate its
   653	readiness to receive more messages.
   654	From the requirements, we can define a formal model, e.g., in
   655	the PROMELA language [110,111], the input language of Spin, as
   656	outlined in the following listing. To simplify our example, we build
   657	the model assuming that the message content cannot be altered and
   658	messages cannot be lost, thus guaranteeing message integrity and order
   659	preservation by construction.
   660	1#define MAX_MESSAGES 10
   661	2chan communication = [MAX_MESSAGES] of
   662	{int};
   663	3chan control_channel = [0] of{bool };4int sent_message = 0;
   664	5int received_message = 0;
   665	6
   666	7proctype Sender( chan sender_to_receiver) {
   667	8 int counter = 0;
   668	9 bool x;
   669	10 do
   670	11 :: counter < MAX_MESSAGES ->
   671	12 sent_message=counter;
   672	13 sender_to_receiver!sent_message;
   673	14 counter++;
   674	15 control_channel? eval (1);
   675	16 ::counter == MAX_MESSAGES ->goto done;
   676	17 od;
   677	18 done: skip ;
   678	19}
   679	20
   680	21proctype Receiver( chan receiver_to_sender) {
   681	22int expected_counter = 0;
   682	23bool check
   683	24do
   684	25 ::expected_counter < MAX_MESSAGES ->
   685	26 receiver_to_sender?
   686	received_message;
   687	27 control_channel!1;
   688	28 expected_counter++;
   689	29 ::expected_counter == MAX_MESSAGES ->
   690	break ;
   691	30od;
   692	31}
   693	32
   694	33init {
   695	34 run Sender(communication);
   696	35 run Receiver(communication);
   697	36}
   698	Listing 1: PROMELA code for Sender and Receiver processes.
   699	The PROMELA model consists of two processes: Sender and Re-
   700	ceiver. The Sender process (ln 7–19) in Listing 1sends messages
   701	to the Receiver process via thesender_to _receiver chan-
   702	nel. This is done through the instruction sender_to_receiver!
   703	sent_message . The process pushes on the channel (with the use of
   704	the symbol !) the current message (sent_message ) (cf. ln 13). Then,
   705	it waits for an acknowledgement from the Receiver (ln 15). This is
   706	achieved with the instruction control_channel?eval(1) , which
   707	is a blocking instruction. Indeed, the channel control_channel is
   708	waiting to read (symbol ?) the value 1(the keyword eval indicates
   709	that the value read on the channel needs to match what is in paren-
   710	thesis, e.g., the value 1). Once it receives it, it creates a new message
   711	and sends it to the receiver. The new message will include the value
   712	of the variable counter , which is used to ensure the order of the
   713	messages, as this will be later controlled by the Receiver. It continues
   714	this loop until it has sent the maximum number of planned messages
   715	(ln 16). The Receiver process (ln 21–31) receives messages from the
   716	Sender process via thereceiver_to_sender channel through
   717	the instruction in ln 26. Note that, in this case, the process reads on
   718	the channel (with the symbol ?) a value and saves it in the variable
   719	received_message . Then, as it has read the message sent by the
   720	Sender, it sends an acknowledgement (the value 1 in ln 27) to the
   721	Sender. Communication between the Sender and Receiver processes is
   722	coordinated through thecontrol_channel .
   723	The initial NL requirements can also be translated into LTL formu-
   724	lae, which can be verified by the Spin model checker on the model in
   725	the form of assertions or never claims. Both assertions and never claims
   726	are used to verify properties, but they differ in scope and purpose.
   727	Assertions are ideal for verifying invariants, such as ensuring that a
   728	variable stays within a certain range. In contrast, never claims define
   729	undesirable global behaviours that the system should never exhibit.
   730
   731	--- Page 7 ---
   732	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
   733	7A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
   734	Unlike  assertions,  never claims  are not tied to specific  lines of code but
   735	instead  describe  system-wide  properties.  Therefore,  assertions  are best
   736	for localised,  immediate  conditions,  while never claims  are used to ver-
   737	ify complex,  system-level  properties  over time. In this case, we want to
   738	state that the Sender  cannot  generate  more than one message  before  the
   739	Receiver  receives  it, i.e., the currently  received  message  is the one cur-
   740	rently generated  by the Sender.  Formally, [](received_message
   741	== sent_message) .
   742	To verify this formula  in PROMELA,  which corresponds  to the Flow
   743	Control  requirement  initially  specified,  we can transform  it into an as-
   744	sertion  with the instruction assert(received_message==sent_
   745	message) which should  be inserted  after the Receiver  reads the
   746	message  from thereceiver_to_sender channel  (i.e., between  ln26
   747	and ln27 in Listing 1). This assertion  has to be true every time the
   748	Receiver  reads a message  to check that the Sender  does not send
   749	another  message  before  the Receiver  reads the current  one. Given the
   750	size of the model,  Spin is able to verify this assertion  using 77 states,
   751	86 transitions,  and 128.730  MB, which is mainly  used to store the hash
   752	table used by the verifier.
   753	The considered  property  is verified  because  the model correctly
   754	implements  a synchronisation  protocol  between  the Sender  and the
   755	Receiver.
   756	Consider  now Listing 2, which is a modification  of the previous  code
   757	in which we eliminate  the control  channel  and the associated  lines of
   758	code.
   759	1#define MAX_MESSAGES  10
   760	2
   761	3chan communication  = [MAX_MESSAGES] of
   762	{int};
   763	4int sent_message  = 0;
   764	5int received_message  = 0;
   765	6
   766	7proctype Sender( chan sender_to_receiver)  {
   767	8 int counter  = 0;
