   260	Instruction fine-tuning, or simply instruction tuning, aims to im-
   261	prove the LLM’s ability to follow instructions expressed in NL and
   262	consists of fine-tuning the LLM to perform instruction-based tasks. In
   263	the more classical LM fine-tuning, the training dataset typically consists
   264	of a data item with associated annotations (e.g., a requirement and its
   265	class). With instruction tuning, the training dataset consists of a NL
   266	instruction, which specifies the task to be addressed (e.g., what is the
   267	class of the following requirement?) and the expected answer (e.g., the
   268	actual class). This has been shown to improve the capability of the LLM
   269	to follow specific instructions [29].
   270	RAG enhances the capability of LLMs to produce accurate and
   271	context-relevant output by incorporating external information in the
   272	generation process. When tasked with a certain prompt, prompt-relevant
   273	information is first retrieved from an external knowledge source. Theoriginal prompt is enhanced with the additional information, and the
   274	LLM is queried with this prompt to produce the desired output. This
   275	enables the possibility to combine the language understanding of LLM
   276	with external, typically domain-specific knowledge.
   277	LoRA is a technique used to adapt large pre-trained language models
   278	to specific tasks or domains without retraining all the model param-
   279	eters. LoRA starts by freezing the weight matrices of the pre-trained
   280	LLM, which means these weights are not updated during the adapta-
   281	tion process. LoRA introduces additional matrices into the model that
   282	are smaller in size compared to the model’s original weights. These
   283	matrices are trainable, meaning that they can be adjusted during the
   284	fine-tuning process based on the task-specific data. The fine-tuning does
   285	not modify the weights of the original LM, while modifies the output
   286	of the large matrices during the forward pass of the neural network.
   287	Finally, distillation, or knowledge distillation, is a technique aimed
   288	at transferring the knowledge and capabilities from a large, complex
   289	model (often referred to as theteacher ) to a smaller, more efficient
   290	model (known as thestudent ). The goal is to enable the student model
   291	to perform as closely as possible to the teacher model while being less
   292	resource-intensive.
   293	Another relevant issue to address is the complexity of certain tasks,
   294	which cannot be addressed with a single LLM, but require multiple
   295	LLMs or external resources. To cope with this problem, LLM agents
   296	have emerged [30]. An LLM agent is a system that not only generates
   297	human-like text but also has additional abilities to handle sophisticated,
   298	multi-step workflows, perform reasoning, remember information, and
   299	use tools or external systems, e.g., through APIs, to complete specific
   300	tasks. In a system based on LLM agents, a main LLM acts as the central
   301	controller or ‘‘brain’’, orchestrating a sequence of actions required to
   302	fulfil a task or user request, leveraging other LLM agents. LLM agents
   303	can decide when to use certain modules (e.g., retrieving stored knowl-
   304	edge or performing numerical calculations) and adjust their approach
   305	based on the task at hand. Frameworks supporting the development of
   306	LLM agents are LangChain.3and AutoGPT.4
   307	LMs and LLMs in RE. NLP techniques in general and LMs, in particular,
   308	have been widely studied and applied in RE since its early days—
   309	the first works appeared already in the ’80s [31]. Typical RE tasks
   310	that have been addressed with the support of LMs are ambiguity and
   311	smell detection [32], tracing [33], classification of requirements [34],
   312	as well as app reviews [35] and issues [36], and other tasks. Early
   313	studies using tf-idf and BoW focused on solutions for tracing and
   314	for requirements retrieval from large repositories. In these studies,
   315	these prototypical LMs were mainly exploited to measure the similarity
   316	between requirements so that trace links could be identified and re-
   317	quirements could be retrieved from existing repositories with the goal
   318	of requirements reuse. Word embeddings were also used, specifically
   319	for the identification of cross-domain ambiguity, i.e., terms that have
   320	different meanings in different domains, and to support requirements
   321	tracing with the aid of recurrent neural networks [33]. BERT-like trans-
   322	formers were first exploited for requirements classification [34,37,38],
   323	and saw applications in a multitude of tasks, including requirements re-
   324	trieval [39], app review analysis [40], and assessment of requirements
   325	completeness [41].
   326	The uptake of LLM-based techniques has been rather slow in RE
   327	compared to other SE subfields [3], although some exploratory work
   328	has been started. Let us consider some relevant contributions. Among
   329	the early works, Jain et al. [4] presents a set of experiments on the us-
   330	age of different LLMs for requirements summarisation from legal texts.
   331	They first generated summaries with the aid of GPT-3 and used them
   332	to fine-tune Pegasus, GPT-2, and other open-source models that do not
   333	require the disclosure of data, as in the case of GPT-3. Then, they eval-
   334	uated the performance of these models on industrial datasets, showing
   335	3https://www.langchain.com/ Last visit 30 Oct., 2024.
   336	4https://autogpt.net/category/autogpt/ . Last visit 30 Oct., 2024.
   337
   338	--- Page 4 ---
   339	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
   340	4A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
   341	that Pegasus outperforms the other options. Marczak et al. [5] use Chat-
   342	GPT to generate user stories reflecting human values. The user stories
   343	are then used as creativity triggers in requirements elicitation sessions.
   344	The evaluation with focus groups shows that the generated user stories
   345	effectively inspire participants to identify value-relevant requirements.
   346	Rodriguez et al. [6] experiment with prompting strategies to support
   347	requirements traceability with the aid of Anthropic’s Claude instant
   348	model. Their exploratory evaluation with the CM1 dataset shows that
   349	small prompt modifications can lead to significant differences in model
   350	outputs.
   351	Some works also explore the potential of LLMs for model generation.
   352	Among them, Chen et al. [7] evaluates the potential of GPT-4 for
   353	generating goal models using the textual grammar for the Goal-oriented
   354	Requirement Language (GRL) based on free-form NL descriptions of the
   355	problem context. Their experiments on four example cases show that
   356	incremental prompts can improve the results, and domain knowledge
   357	is highly needed to assess the plausible—yet frequently incorrect—
   358	output. Chen et al. [42] evaluates GPT-3.5 and GPT-4 for generating
   359	class diagrams based on NL descriptions. Their evaluation on a ground
   360	truth from an undergraduate course shows that the LLMs struggle to
   361	generate relationships between classes. Furthermore, they show that
   362	while few-shot approaches (i.e., adding examples to the prompts) can
   363	improve performance, chain-of-thought prompting (i.e., adding rea-
   364	soning steps) leads to a performance decrease. Still on class diagram
   365	generation, Camára et al. [8] perform an exploratory study using
   366	ChatGPT. They exercise the model with different sample problems
   367	and provide some lessons learned. The authors conclude that iterative
   368	prompting is needed to produce models of sufficient quality, and adher-
   369	ence to the standard in terms of construct semantics tends to be poor.
   370	Finally, the study of Ferrari et al. [9] explores the capability of ChatGPT
   371	to generate UML sequence diagrams from requirements. The authors
   372	observed that, although the models generally conform to the standard
   373	and exhibit a reasonable level of understandability, their correctness
   374	with respect to the specified requirements often presents challenges.
   375	This issue is particularly pronounced in the presence of requirements
   376	smells, such as ambiguity and inconsistency.
   377	Besides these initial efforts, recent months have seen substantial
   378	growth in the literature in the area of LLMs for RE, as is evident
   379	from the accepted papers at RE@Next! 2024, which includes seven
   380	papers about LLMs out of 19 contributions, and at RE 2024, which
   381	includes eight papers out of 14 contributions.5These papers cover a
   382	variety of RE tasks through the use of LLMs, from an approach that
   383	uses GPT for automating text completion of requirements within the
   384	context of the specification [43] to the use of in-context learning to
   385	support the evaluation of satisfaction arguments, which describe how
   386	a requirement is satisfied by a system specification [44]. Concerning
   387	the task of requirements elicitation, Gorer et al. propose an approach
   388	that uses LLMs to generate interview scripts [45]. The use of LLMs is
   389	also proposed to elicit and specify SRS documents [46] and check the
   390	quality of requirements [47]. Additional exploratory studies investigate
   391	the use of LLMs to generate code from requirements [48] or to improve
   392	the representation of normative requirements [49].
   393	2.2. Formal methods and formal RE
   394	When formal techniques are applied, the system requirements are
   395	represented through a formal specification that defines ‘‘what’’ the
   396	system should do and the constraints it must operate under (e.g., [50]).
   397	Then, either this specification is directly analysed, or the system is
   398	modelled through a state-based formalism, which describes ‘‘how’’
   399	the system operates (e.g., [51]) and then analysed against properties
   400	extracted from the specification and often represented in mathematical
   401	logic (e.g., [52]). In the following, we will present some of the most
   402	developed and used formal techniques to specify requirements, create
   403	models, and analyse them.
   404	5https://conf.researchr.org/home/re-2024 . Last Visit 29 Oct. 2024.Formal representation of specifications. FMs for requirements specifica-
   405	tion were first used in the 1970s to cope with the need for more
   406	rigorous and precise software development methods, given the increas-
   407	ing complexity and critical use of software systems in fields such as
   408	aerospace, medical, and military domains. In addition, high-profile
   409	software failures (e.g., the partial meltdown of the Three Mile Island
   410	nuclear power plant in Pennsylvania [53]) highlighted the limitations
   411	of conventional development techniques and the need for more precise
   412	and verifiable approaches to software development.
   413	An example of formal languages for specifications is theZ Nota-
   414	tion[54,55], which uses set theory and first-order predicate logic to
   415	specify system properties and behaviours. The core construct in the Z
   416	notation is the schema, which groups related state variables and their
   417	invariants together. Schemas can be used to describe both the state of
   418	a system and the operations that can be performed on that state. They
   419	can also be composed to support modularity, allowing complex systems
   420	to be described in a structured and hierarchical manner.
   421	Analogously to the Z notation, theVienna Development Model (VDM)
   422	[56] provides a model-based approach that simplifies the design and
   423	verification of complex systems. VDM uses formal specification lan-
   424	guages based on mathematical notation to describe the system’s func-
   425	tionality, structure, and constraints. The most commonly used speci-
   426	fication languages in VDM are VDM Specification Language [57] and
   427	VDM++ [58], an object-oriented extension of VDM-SL. VDM crates
   428	mathematical models as blueprints for the system, capturing its es-
   429	sential properties and behaviours. VDM emphasises the use of formal
   430	proofs to verify that the system’s implementation satisfies its specifica-
   431	tions. Proof obligations are generated to demonstrate that the system is
   432	correct with the support of tools like VDMTools [59] and Overture [60].
   433	Differently from the Z notation and VDM, which primarily focus
   434	on providing a formal specification language, theB-Method [61] is a
   435	comprehensive formal method covering specification, refinement, and
   436	implementation with a strong emphasis on proofs and tool support.
   437	The core concepts in the B-Method are abstract machines, which rep-
   438	resent components or modules of a system, define the state of the
   439	system through variables, and specify operations that can change this
   440	state. The operations are specified through preconditions that must
   441	be true before an operation can be executed and postconditions that
   442	describe the state after the operation has been executed. The B-Method
   443	allows transforming an abstract specification into a more concrete one,
   444	step by step, until an executable implementation is achieved through
   445	subsequent refinements.
   446	In addition to these approaches, over the last three decades, a
   447	variety of languages have been introduced. Process algebras such as CCS
   448	(Calculus of Communicating Systems) [62] and CSP (Communicating
   449	Sequential Processes) [63] are formal languages used to describe and
   450	analyse the behaviour of concurrent systems. The Specification and
   451	Description Language (SDL) [64] is another formal language specifically
   452	designed for describing complex, event-driven, real-time systems, com-
   453	monly used in telecommunications and other industries. The Common
   454	Algebraic Specification Language (CASL) [65] andLanguage of Temporal
   455	Ordering Specification (LOTOS) [66], including its extended version E-
   456	LOTOS, are algebraic and process-based languages used for specifying
   457	and verifying concurrent and distributed systems. Temporal Logic of Ac-
   458	tions (TLA+) [67] andAlloy [68] are used for specifying and modelling
   459	systems, with Alloy focusing on relational logic and TLA+ on temporal
   460	logic. Finally, some specification languages are inspired by program-
   461	ming languages or natural languages. For example, FizzBee [69] is
   462	a Python-inspired design specification language. Attempto Controlled
   463	English (ACE) [70] is a controlled natural language that allows spec-
   464	ifications to be written in a subset of English, aiming to reduce the
   465	complexity of formal methods for non-expert users.
   466
   467	--- Page 5 ---
   468	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
   469	5A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
   470	Representing the properties of the system. The properties that a specifica-
   471	tion has to satisfy (e.g., safety or liveness) are usually represented using
   472	some (temporal) extension of propositional or predicate logic [71].
   473	Linear Temporal Logic (LTL) [72] is an extension of propositional logic,
   474	widely used for specifying properties over linear sequences of states.
   475	LTL is particularly effective for expressing safety and liveness prop-
   476	erties, which describe what must or must not happen during system
   477	execution. The syntax of LTL includes temporal operators such as X
   478	(next), F or⟨⟩(eventually), G or [] (globally), and U (until). Compu-
   479	tation Tree Logic (CTL) [73] is another temporal logic used to specify
   480	properties over branching structures of states, which allows for the
   481	expression of properties concerning possible future states. It combines
   482	path quantifiers A (for all paths) and E (there exists a path) with the
   483	same temporal operators used in LTL. In CTL, path quantifiers cannot
   484	be used independently from temporal operators, and this makes LTL’s
   485	and CTL’s expressiveness incomparable. Instead, CTL∗[74] combines
   486	both LTL and CTL, allowing more complex specifications by enabling
   487	both path quantifiers and temporal operators to be mixed freely. 𝜇-
   488	calculus [75] is another powerful formalism that extends temporal
   489	logics like LTL and CTL by introducing fixed-point operators. This
   490	makes it one of the most expressive logics for specifying properties of
   491	transition systems, enabling the expression of complex properties like
   492	those involving recursion.
   493	Higher-order logic (HOL) [76] extends predicate logic by allowing
   494	quantification over predicates and functions, providing a more ex-
   495	pressive framework for specifying and verifying complex properties.
   496	Additional logical formalisms used to specify properties include Modal
   497	Logic [77],Metric (MTL) and Real-Time Temporal Logic (RTTL) [78],
   498	and Probabilistic Temporal Logics (e.g., Probabilistic Computation Tree
   499	Logic [79]). Modal Logic includes modalities such as necessity and
   500	possibility to express properties about different modes of truth, such
   501	as ‘‘necessarily true’’ or ‘‘possibly true’’. This logic is useful in systems
   502	where different states or conditions can coexist, and properties need to
   503	be expressed concerning these modes. MTL and RTTL extend LTL with
   504	timing constraints, allowing the specification of properties in real-time
   505	systems. Probabilistic Temporal Logics are used to specify properties
   506	of probabilistic systems, incorporating probabilities into the logical
   507	framework.
   508	Formal models. Labelled Transition Systems (LTSs) [79] are widely used
   509	to model the behaviour of concurrent systems. An LTS consists of
   510	states and transitions between these states, where each transition is
   511	labelled with an action. Each state represents a configuration of the
   512	system, and the transition shows how to move from one configuration
   513	to another. Analogously, Finite State Machines (FSMs) [73] are used to
   514	model systems with a finite number of states and transitions between
   515	those states. The main difference between the two models is that
   516	FSMs use input symbols to determine state transitions, while LTSs
   517	use labelled actions that can represent a broader range of events or
   518	interactions. In addition, LTSs are well-suited for modelling concurrent
   519	systems and their interactions, whereas FSMs are more straightforward
   520	and often used for sequential logic. A particularly used type of FSMs
   521	isBüchi Automata [51,80], used for modelling and verifying properties
   522	of infinite sequences. These automata, as FSMs, have a finite number
   523	of states, but thanks to their acceptance condition, they can describe
   524	infinite behaviours.
   525	FSMs can also be extended to include time and probability. Timed
   526	Automata (TAs) [78] extend FSMs with clocks, allowing the modelling
   527	of real-time systems. The clock variables are used to measure the
   528	passage of time and to set guards on the transitions. TAs enable the
   529	specification of timing requirements such as deadlines, periodicity, and
   530	delays. Probabilistic and Stochastic State Machines extend the classical
   531	FSMs by incorporating probabilistic and stochastic elements. These ex-
   532	tensions enable modelling systems that exhibit random or probabilistic
   533	behaviour, which is crucial when systems operate under uncertainty.
   534	Probabilistic State Machines [81] introduce probabilities into the statetransition mechanism by regulating transitions between states using
   535	predefined probabilities. Differently, Stochastic State Machines [82]
   536	incorporate timing aspects into the probabilistic state transitions, often
   537	using stochastic processes like Markov chains.
   538	The need to describe the hierarchical structure of software has
   539	led to the introduction of different hierarchical models, such asStat-
   540	echarts [83]. Statecharts extend FSMs by incorporating concepts of
   541	hierarchy, concurrency, and communication. They allow the definition
   542	of hierarchical states that are refined by substates to capture different
   543	levels of abstraction. In addition, they model concurrency through
   544	parallel states. Other hierarchical models include Hierarchical State
   545	Machines [84],MODELICA State Machines [85], and Ptolemy II State
   546	Machines [86].
   547	A commonly used tool to model a system isPetri Nets (PNs) [87],
   548	a graphical and mathematical modelling tool used to describe the flow
   549	of information and control in systems, particularly in concurrent and
   550	distributed systems. PNs used places to represent conditions or states
   551	of the system and transitions to represent events causing changes in
   552	conditions. To show that a condition is met (or the system is in a
   553	state), they use tokens that allow transitions to be executed. Analo-
   554	gously to FSMs, PNs have been extended with different attributes to
   555	make them more suitable to represent complex systems. For example,
   556	Colored PNs[88] introduce colours to tokens, enabling the modelling
   557	of systems with different types of resources; Timed PNs[89] incorpo-
   558	rate timing information into PNs, allowing transitions to have firing
   559	delays; Stochastic PNs[90] extend PNs with the probabilistic firing of
   560	transitions, incorporating uncertainty into the model; andHierarchical
   561	PNs[91] introduce hierarchy into PNs by allowing places or transitions
   562	to contain subnets.
   563	Formal methods for analysing models. There exists a variety of methods
   564	to analyse and verify the formalisms presented in this section or directly
   565	code. Given the expressive power of such formalisms and the complex-
   566	ity of the verification problem, each technique has different advantages
   567	and limitations as they need to compromise between the expressivity
   568	of the input languages and automation.
   569	Abstract Interpretation [92] uses the idea that the verification of
   570	the considered system can be done at a high level of abstraction,
   571	ignoring irrelevant details. Techniques such as interval analysis and
   572	polyhedral analysis are used to approximate the behaviour of the
   573	system. Astrèe [93] and Infer [94] are examples of tools that leverage
   574	abstract interpretation to detect potential runtime errors, such as buffer
   575	overflows and division by zero, in critical software systems.
   576	Semantic Static Analysis [95] offers a complete and automatic anal-
   577	ysis of a program’s source code without running it by using data
   578	flow analysis, control flow analysis, and type checking. This approach
   579	is supported by tools such as Coverity [96], PVS-Studio [97], and
   580	FindBugs [98] that help detect software defects and vulnerabilities and
   581	ensure compliance with coding standards by analysing source code for
   582	potential issues.
   583	Model Checking [73] verifies specifications on different types of
   584	models of the system by complete exploration. The most commonly
   585	used approaches are state space exploration and symbolic model check-
   586	ing. Spin [99] is an example of an automata-based model checker that
   587	takes as input a system modelled as a Büchi Automaton (described
   588	in PROMELA, Spin input language) and a property expressed in LTL.
   589	NuSMV [100] (and its evolution nuXmv) is a symbolic model checker
   590	for the formal verification of finite-state systems, expressed in LTS,
   591	against properties specified in CTL. UPPAAL [101], instead, is used for
   592	modelling, simulation, and verification of real-time systems.
   593	Proof Assistants are interactive tools that allow users to write proofs
   594	of theorems and are used both in mathematics and computer sci-
   595	ence. Techniques include inductive proof, coinductive proof, and auto-
   596	mated theorem proving. Tools like Coq [102], Isabelle/HOL [103], and
   597	Agda [104] assist in developing formal proofs by providing a frame-
   598	work for constructing and checking proofs and have been used in veri-
   599	fying software correctness, cryptographic protocols, and mathematical
   600
   601	--- Page 6 ---
   602	Information and Software Technology 181 (2025) 107697
   603	6A. Ferrari and P. Spoletini
   604	theorems.
   605	Deductive Verification [13] involves providing a program and a
   606	formal specification, which is then propagated through the source code
   607	of the program, using the weakest preconditions and symbolic execu-
   608	tion. Techniques include Hoare logic, weakest precondition calculus,
   609	and predicate transformers. Examples of tools that support deductive
   610	verification are Frama-C [105], a platform dedicated to the analysis of
   611	the source code of software written in C, Dafny [106], a verification-
   612	aware programming language that allows specifications to be written
   613	in line with the code, and KeYKeY [107], a formal verification tool for
   614	Java programs.
   615	Design by Refinement [108] is an approach based on successive
